	abstract = {Machine Learning (ML) has significantly enhanced the forecasting accuraacy of wind energy systems. However, the black-box character of ML models raises questions about transparency, reliability, and security which are the essential elements in maintaining stakeholder confidence in AI-driven renewable energy systems. This research establishes an interpretable wind power forecasting framework that combines Random Forest predictions with SHAP-based explainability and security-oriented validation to effectively address these challenges. Utilizing SCADA data from a 1.5 MW onshore wind turbine (Sinovel SL1500/90, Yalova, Turkey), the framework demonstrates exceptional forecasting accuracy (R²=0.9233, RMSE=344.89 kW, MAE=167.07 kW) surpassing AdaBoost, SVR, KNN, and LASSO by 0.41–10.87% across comprehensive expanding window cross-validation. SHAP analysis identifies wind speed as the main predictor with minimal variability (CV=5.2%, SRCC=1.0). In contrast wind direction exhibits greater fluctuations (CV=37.3%) despite a stable ranking, thus guiding sensor maintenance prioritization. Three components designed to enhance security reinforce operational deployment: (i) feature stability analysis that quantifies predictor consistency across temporal partitions; (ii) SHAP-enhanced iForest achieving the lowest FPR (4.93%) among five anomaly detection methods, thereby reducing unnecessary dispatches by 21% and providing interpretable fault diagnostics; and (iii) adversarial robustness validation confirming a performance retention of 95.76% (R²=0.8842) under realistic sensor noise conditions (±0.75 m/s, ±10.8°), with SHAP attribution stability (ASS=120.44 kW) ensuring consistent interpretability. A comprehensive ablation study confirms the contribution of each component, while a practical use case illustrates a 75% reduction in diagnostic time for yaw misalignment detection. The framework converts black-box predictions into transparent, actionable decision-support, facilitating more efficient defect diagnosis, predictive maintenance planning, and energy loss mitigation achieving a higher benefit-cost ratio with quantifiable operational benefits for wind farm stakeholders. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
