	abstract = {The increasing complexity of power system asset management, driven by aging infrastructure, volatile operational conditions, and rising reliability expectations, calls for new frameworks that unify predictive analytics with robust decision-making. This paper proposes an integrated optimization architecture for predictive maintenance and spare parts inventory control, and underground cables. At its core, the model couples degradation dynamics with inventory logistics by embedding a hybrid Remaining Useful Life (RUL) forecasting engine—comprising both Wiener-process-based physics models. The inventory control problem is formulated as a multi-objective, mixed-integer program under distributional uncertainty in RUL forecasts. Additionally, a Deep Q-Network (DQN) reinforcement learning module is trained to adaptively determine spare part dispatch decisions based on evolving state information, including forecasted RUL, current inventory levels, and part criticality. A bilevel optimization mechanism links predictive model calibration and downstream inventory outcomes, enabling joint learning and cost-aware forecasting. The proposed framework is validated through a case study involving 75 assets and 4 depots over a 24-month planning horizon, with real-time sensor emulation and delivery delays. Results demonstrate that the integrated approach achieves up to 72% reduction in emergency maintenance events and 34% cost savings relative to periodic baselines, while exceeding 90% protection coverage. Copyright © 2025. Published by Elsevier Ltd.},
