	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031677590?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The global transition to renewable energy requires enhanced predictive capabilities to address climate change challenges and energy security concerns. This study investigates the application of a Random Forest model for predicting renewable energy generation at a solar power system in China's Tengger Desert, examining how environmental factors affect operational efficiency within the observed conditions. The model achieved strong performance across training and testing datasets, with R<sup>2</sup> scores of 0.9982 and 0.9979, and Mean Absolute Error values of 0.6385 MWh and 0.8566 MWh, respectively. Scenario analysis revealed that a 10% reduction in global irradiation produced a modest decrease in energy output (−0.13%), while a 5 °C temperature increase showed minimal effects within the tested range. SHAP analysis identified solar irradiance and average power output as the most significant predictors of energy generation among the variables examined. These results suggest a potential reconsideration of conventional temperature-focused design priorities and offer insights for system optimization, though findings are constrained by reliance on historical data from a single location. The analysis provides valuable insights into environmental-energy relationships that may inform policy development and energy planning when integrated with broader system considerations, validated across diverse operational contexts. While the predictive framework demonstrates promise for renewable energy forecasting, generalization beyond the study parameters requires additional research incorporating unmeasured environmental factors, technological variations, and diverse geographical conditions to ensure reliable application in varied deployment scenarios. © 2026},
