	abstract = {The increasing trend of Electric Vehicles (EVs) has both opportunities and challenges to the contemporary power systems, especially where it is incorporated with alternative sources of energy such as solar photovoltaics. Although literature on Reinforcement Learning (RL) as a method to optimize EV charging has already been conducted, most studies are limited to the case of a single station and ignore spatial organization and solar variability. The proposed paper suggests a new Hierarchical Deep Reinforcement Learning (HDRL) model that would allow coordinated EV charging to be performed even in geographically dispersed solar-powered stations. The high-level controller assigns quota of energy to any specific station as per real-time solar forecasting, and the low-level agents take charge of making local EV charging choices to make as much solar use as possible, as little delay as possible, and less reliance on the grid. To confirm our methodology, we use real-life data of two solar generating plants that have generation and weather sensor data. We initially use Machine Learning (ML) models namely the Random Forest and XGBoost to predict short-term solar power with the highest R 2 of 0.995. Another aspect that we simulate to model multi-station coordination during changeable weather conditions is realistic EV demand profiles. Initial findings indicate the consistency of our HDRL plan in enhancing power usage, loading optimization and sustainability in distributed charging systems. The paper establishes a framework of scalable smart grid EV fleet management that is solar-conscious. © 2026 IEEE.},
