	abstract = {Efficient catalysts for hydrogen generation from NaBH<sub>4</sub> hydrolysis are crucial for advancing sustainable energy systems. In this study, a carbon-confined biphasic CoMoP/CoP heterostructured catalyst (NN-CMP/CP) was synthesized via an etching—ion exchange—phosphorization strategy, utilizing nickel foam-anchored triangular plates as a template. The interconnected 3D network, enhanced by the carbon matrix, facilitates electron transfer and mass transport, while synergistic interfacial effects between CoMoP and CoP significantly boost hydrogen evolution kinetics. Systematic single-factor experiments clarified the individual effects of key operational parameters on hydrogen generation rates. Furthermore, response surface methodology (RSM) revealed significant interaction effects between NaBH<sub>4</sub> and NaOH concentrations, as well as between NaOH concentration and catalyst mass. The optimized conditions (2 wt% NaBH<sub>4</sub>, 5 wt% NaOH, 22.58 mg catalyst) resulted in a maximum hydrogen generation rate of 5632 mL min−1 g−1, with experimental validation showing a relative error of only 2.6%. The integration of carbon confinement, biphasic synergy, and RSM-guided optimization highlights NN-CMP/CP as a highly promising non-noble metal catalyst for efficient and tunable hydrogen production. © 2026 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
