	abstract = {With the advancement of deep learning in spatiotemporal sequence modeling, Convolutional Long Short-Term Memory (ConvLSTM) networks have shown strong potential in environmental risk assessment. This paper proposes a ConvLSTM-based algorithm for flood disaster assessment in substations, aiming to capture both spatial dependencies and temporal dynamics of hydrometeorological factors. The model integrates multi-source data - including precipitation forecasts, digital elevation models (DEM), land cover types, and historical flood records - to predict flood risks and identify vulnerable substations. A spatiotemporal data pipeline is constructed to preprocess and input the fused data into the ConvLSTM network for end-to-end training and prediction. Experimental validation was conducted on real-world datasets from five flood-prone regions in southern China, covering 20 substations with diverse geographical features. Results show that the proposed model achieves a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 0.072, an Intersection over Union (IoU) of 0.843, and an F1-score of 0.879, outperforming baseline models such as standard LSTM and Random Forest in both spatial accuracy and temporal stability. These findings demonstrate that the ConvLSTM-based approach offers a robust and accurate solution for substation flood disaster assessment, providing valuable technical support for early warning systems and disaster resilience planning in power grids.CCS CONCEPT•Computing methodologiesg1/4Machine learningg1/4Machine learning approachesg1/4Neural networks  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
