	abstract = {This study explores load forecasting, a crucial aspect of smart grid research, which includes various approaches like traditional time-series econometric analysis, contemporary deep learning, and machine learning methods. Enhanced prediction accuracy is increasingly sought after in various sectors, from individual consumers to the broader power grid. This demand is fueled by advancements in the energy market and the widespread adoption of smart grid technology. Furthermore, the inclusion of electric vehicles introduces another aspect to the requirement for accurate prediction. In addition, accurate energy prediction presents difficulties because of external factors such as changes in population, weather patterns, and seasonal fluctuations. This research introduces a unique machine learning framework that is specifically tailored to predict short-term time series load. The ensemble combines various base learners such as Support Vector Machines (SVM), Gradient Boosting (GB), and Random Forests (RF) models. These models are integrated using stacking, along with ”Elastic net” linear regression. The model hyperparameters are fine-tuned meticulously utilizing a combination of GridSearchCV and cross-validation. Additionally, the SVM algorithm is optimized using Bayesian optimization. Feature engineering and selection entail evaluating the significance of time series data, encompassing load and weather data. The validation of model performance is carried out in a systematic manner, utilizing measurements such as mean absolute error (MAE), root mean square error (RMSE), and mean absolute percentage error (MAPE). The empirical findings clearly show that the suggested method is capable of providing accurate and reliable forecasts predicate on the hourly electrical energy consumption of the IEEE dataset. This highlights its effectiveness in the field of short-term load prediction. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
