	abstract = {The rapid growth in electric vehicle (EV) adoption has introduced new challenges for energy management and power grid stability due to the variability and unpredictability of EV charging demand. Accurate short-term load forecasting is essential for optimizing charging infrastructure, preventing grid congestion, and supporting sustainable mobility initiatives. This study investigates various data driven forecasting models such as Long Short Term Memory (LSTM) and Support Vector Regression (SVR) for predicting EV charging load using real world data from public charging stations in Palo Alto, California. The dataset was preprocessed to create a time series representation of aggregated hourly power demand. Extensive feature engineering was performed, incorporating temporal indicators, lagged power values, rolling statistics, and meteorological variables. The models were evaluated using MAE, RMSE, MAPE, and Filtered MAPE to assess their performance, particularly under high-load conditions. The results show that the LSTM model significantly outperforms SVR across all evaluation metrics, demonstrating superior capability in capturing temporal dependencies and non-linear charging behavior. SVR under performed due to its limited sequence modeling capacity. The findings highlight the effectiveness of deep learning methods for EV load forecasting and offer insights for future infrastructure planning and intelligent energy management systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
