	abstract = {Driven by global energy transition and carbon neutrality goals, artificial intelligence presents a paradigm-shifting opportunity for innovation in energy storage technologies. This paper systematically reviews the technical framework and innovative applications of AI in the energy storage field, with a focus on analyzing its core value in material design, identification and screening, performance prediction, and empowering smart grids and renewable energy management. The results demonstrate that AI, through machine learning algorithms such as random forests, support vector machines, and neural networks, can efficiently screen high-capacity electrode materials and optimize nanocomposite dielectrics, reducing the material discovery cycle from years to months. In terms of performance prediction, convolutional neural networks and reinforcement learning frameworks can reduce battery life prediction errors to within 2.4% and achieve accurate simulation of the dynamic behavior of thermal energy storage systems. At the system level, AI utilizes LSTM models to forecast renewable energy output and optimize energy storage dispatch, significantly enhancing the grid's capacity to absorb variable renewable energy and improving operational economics. This paper aims to construct a full-chain analytical framework spanning from “microscopic materials to mesoscopic devices and macroscopic systems,” placing explainable artificial intelligence at the core of the review for the first time, to systematically elaborate its key role in enhancing the intelligence, efficiency, and sustainability of energy storage systems. The goal is to provide methodological support for advancing energy storage technologies toward low-cost, high-reliability, and intelligent development. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
