	abstract = {Early identification of transformer faults is essential for the secure and stable operation of power systems. Dissolved Gas Analysis (DGA) is one of the most widely adopted techniques for diagnosing internal faults in transformers, where temporal variations in key gases such as hydrogen, methane, and ethylene are closely associated with fault types. However, existing DGA gas prediction models often suffer from redundant input features and insufficient hyperparameter tuning, which limits their prediction accuracy and robustness. To address these limitations, this study proposes a multi-gas prediction method that integrates feature compression and parameter optimization. First, a recursive feature elimination (RFE) strategy is designed by combining the Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator (Lasso) with feature importance (FI) and Random Forest (RF), allowing effective identification of informative features from high-dimensional time-series data. Second, an Improved Grey Wolf Optimizer (IGWO) is employed to perform cooperative optimization of the structural parameters of the Random Forest model. These components are integrated into a unified prediction framework, referred to as IGWO-LRFE-FI-RF. Experimental results on real-world datasets demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms several state-of-the-art models in daily-level prediction tasks of multiple DGA gases, exhibiting superior generalization and practical applicability. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
