	doi = {10.1016/j.scs.2026.107369},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035293558?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {City-scale assessments of future building energy demand often rely on standard meteorological datasets that do not reflect urban thermal amplification, potentially underestimating cooling-dominated energy growth in dense cities. However, the extent to which projected climate warming alters city-scale energy use intensity (EUI) under urban thermal conditions remains insufficiently understood. Focusing on Nanjing's historic urban core, this study examines climate-driven changes in building energy use intensity under CMIP6 Shared Socioeconomic Pathways. Future meteorological inputs were bias-corrected and downscaled from 10 CMIP6 projections and adjusted to represent urban thermal conditions. Building energy simulations were conducted using an archetype-based EnergyPlus workflow, and city-scale results were aggregated according to the structural composition of the building stock. Nonlinear relationships between climatic variables, urban form, and energy growth were further examined using a Gradient Boosting Regression model with SHAP-based interpretation. Results indicate that annual mean air temperature is projected to rise by 3.7–4.2 °C by the 2080s, with higher values under urban-adjusted conditions. Total city-scale energy use intensity increases by 9%–13% under SSP2-4.5 and exceeds 20% under SSP5-8.5, primarily driven by a 15%–30% increase in cooling demand alongside declining heating demand. High-load commercial and hotel buildings dominate absolute growth, whereas residential buildings exhibit greater climatic sensitivity. Nonlinear analysis reveals threshold responses to extreme temperature and morphological characteristics, highlighting the interaction between projected warming and urban form. These findings provide quantitative evidence of climate-driven energy growth under urban thermal conditions and offer insights to support adaptive urban energy planning and retrofit prioritisation. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
