	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035903401?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Thermal management is a major reliability bottleneck in three-dimensional integrated circuits (3D-ICs), where strong inter-tier thermal non-uniformity limits the effectiveness of conventional peripheral sensing. To address this issue, this paper proposes TSV-ThermSense, a design-technology co-optimization (DTCO) framework for low-intrusion in-situ inter-tier temperature monitoring by reusing redundant through-silicon vias (TSVs) as a series thermoresistive sensing network. At the device level, we build a deep neural network (DNN)-based surrogate response-surface model and perform multi-objective optimization over sensitivity, power-temperature coefficient, thermal time constant, and hotspot resolvability to obtain Pareto-optimal design trade-offs. At the system level, we combine convolution-based thermal-response modeling with K-means clustering to identify peak and average temperature sensitive regions (PTSRs/ATSRs), and then co-optimize placement under a fixed sensor budget. Across multiple power and hotspot scenarios, the proposed strategy keeps the inter-tier average temperature monitoring error within 0.1 K, while the peak-temperature error is below 3.0 K in the worst case (2.82 K) and can reach 0.0342 K in favorable cases. The Pareto front provides representative operating points, including a low-power design with P<sub>tempco</sub>≈0.22μW/K, a high-sensitivity design with S>21μV/K, and a fast response design with τ≈0.1ms. To ensure implementation feasibility, we incorporate 12-bit ADC/readout-noise constraints into the DTCO flow. Within a 10–80 K regional temperature rise range, the minimum TSV count exceeds 9 for a ±0.5% accuracy target in low temperature rise regions and decreases to 8 for a ±1% target at 10 K; for the ±1% case, the requirement stabilizes near 50K. Raising the SNR target from 4 to 8 dB further increases the minimum TSV requirement from 5 to more than 8, demonstrating a closed-loop path from thermal observability to circuit-constrained sensor design. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
