	abstract = {In actual cloud service application scenarios, user needs are diverse and complex, usually with high uncertainty and sensitivity to indicators such as time and service quality, Cloud platforms in power systems have unique requirements for resource elasticity, which makes it a huge challenge for cloud service providers to accurately predict future needs. Elastic scaling is an effective means to solve this problem and is also the key to achieving a balance between efficient resource utilization and service quality. In view of the resource waste and response lag problems of traditional elastic scaling methods, this paper proposes a dynamic scaling method based on multi-dimensional feature analysis and quantitative uncertainty. Specifically, this method uses the advantages of the Transformer model in time series prediction to collect and process multi-dimensional information, so as to accurately capture the long-term dependency and short-term correlation between demand data and performance indicators. Secondly, the Gaussian process regression model is used to model the complex historical time series data features, so as to quantify the uncertainty of demand and realize accurate prediction of future user demand, and make corresponding adjustments to the computing resource pool. Experiments show that this method can effectively reduce service quality fluctuations while reducing operating costs, and has high practical value.  © COPYRIGHT SPIE. Downloading of the abstract is permitted for personal use only.},
