	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105030214361?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {As the prospect of establishing a sustainable human settlement on Mars becomes increasingly tangible, ensuring a reliable and efficient energy supply represents a fundamental challenge. In this study, supercritical carbon dioxide Brayton cycle combined heat and power systems using space molten salt reactor as the primary heat source were designed to meet the dual demands of electricity and heating on the Martian surface. Four supercritical CO<sub>2</sub> Brayton cycle combined heat and power system configurations—simple, reheat, recompression, and reheat–recompression—were comparatively analyzed using comprehensive thermodynamic and mass estimation models. A hybrid back propagation neural network–Kriging surrogate model was developed to accurately predict system performance parameters, including total energy efficiency, thermal efficiency, exergy efficiency, total mass, specific mass, and heating-to-power ratio. Multi-objective optimization targeting the simultaneous maximization of total energy efficiency and thermal efficiency and minimization of total mass was performed using the non-dominated sorting genetic algorithm, followed by decision-making through the technique for order preference by similarity to ideal solution analysis. The results indicated that the recompression Brayton cycle cogeneration configuration achieved the most favorable performance, with an optimal total energy efficiency of 75.84%, a thermal efficiency of 33.44%, a system mass of 17,429.57 kg, and a heating-to-power ratio of 1.21. The proposed back propagation neural network–Kriging hybrid model exhibited superior prediction accuracy and generalization capability compared with standalone Kriging, effectively supporting high-fidelity system optimization. This work provides a robust methodological framework and practical design reference for next-generation nuclear-powered Mars surface energy systems integrating advanced surrogate modeling and multi-objective optimization. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
