	doi = {10.1016/j.rineng.2025.106286},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010933602?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The constrained optimization of autothermal reforming (ATR) processes involving multiple conflicting objectives, such as operational expenditure (OPEX), carbon footprint, and hydrogen production, remains a critical challenge in process systems engineering. Although conventional multi-objective optimization techniques have been widely applied to hydrogen production systems, their reliance on high computational budgets and limited integration with intelligent learning frameworks constrains their scalability and adaptability. In this study, a Surrogate-assisted Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning (S-MORL) framework is developed to achieve the simultaneous minimization of OPEX and CO₂ emissions while enhancing hydrogen production within the ATR process. The approach integrates tabular Q-learning with a random forest-based surrogate model to accelerate objective evaluations during policy training, significantly improving sample efficiency and reducing dependence on computationally expensive reactor simulations. Surrogate-guided exploration enables efficient navigation of the multi-objective space while maintaining constraint feasibility, including a temperature constraint to prevent catalyst deactivation due to thermal degradation. This integration facilitates interpretable learning dynamics and reliable convergence without the need for complex neural architectures or large-scale training data. The resulting solutions demonstrate notable improvements across all targeted criteria. The proposed framework helped reduce the simulation cost and also achieved 65.69 % higher hypervolume and 66.26 % lower IGD than the genetic algorithm. Performance evaluation using the TOPSIS method yielded a score of 0.9699, indicating a high level of proximity to the ideal solution and reflecting the framework's effectiveness in navigating trade-offs among environmental, economic, and process performance goals. The proposed S-MORL framework offers a promising and scalable approach for the sustainable intensification of hydrogen production in autothermal reforming and other industrial energy systems. © 2025 The Author(s)},
