	abstract = {This article provides designers with experimental and real-time optimization of an innovative solar inverter system using high-purity 4H-SiC substrates grown through the Physical Vapor Transport (PVT) technique. The synthesized 4H-SiC wafers had a 32% lower dislocation density and a 28% reduction in the XRD full width at half maximum (FWHM) compared to normal SiC substrates. With these structural improvements, an 18.4 °C decrease in junction temperature and a 27% increase in mean time between failures (MTBF) were achieved and confirmed by Arrhenius modeling. Electrical characterization revealed an inverter efficiency exceeding 98%. Switching losses and thermal stress were reduced by 23% without requiring a cooled environment, using silicon-based inverters. A monitoring architecture was implemented using IoT to acquire parameters multiple times in real time, achieving 98% data continuity even under weak connectivity and high field temperatures. All data gathered, representing 1480 samples from a 12-day full-spectrum operation, were trained on a Random Forest (RF) regression model. These results indicate that the model’s R<sup>2</sup> was 0.957 (test data) and 0.945 (unseen conditions); the mean absolute error (MAE) was less than 2.0%. The feature importance analysis revealed that junction temperature (32.4%) and PV power input (24.6%) are the most significant influences on inverter efficiency. The edge computing strategies ensured a latency of less than 240 ms and a computational load of no more than 25%, demonstrating adequate real-time adaptability. Its integrated framework helped reduce inverter downtime under variable irradiance and load conditions by minimizing energy losses. The results justify the synergy of ultra-pure 4H-SiC materials with intelligent IoT-ML systems and provide a way to achieve scalable, cost-effective, and reliable power conversion units suitable for future smart-grid and microgrid applications. © 2026 The Authors.},
