	abstract = {This article presents a comprehensive assessment of the techno-economic efficiency of integrating photovoltaic (PV) systems, wind turbines (WT), and battery energy storage (BES) with a drain water heat recovery (DWHR) system in a building. The analysis is based on computer simulations performed with a 5-min time step, covering 513 cases and a 25-year system lifetime. The optimization of the energy system configuration was carried out using a genetic algorithm (GA). Subsequently, predictive models based on machine learning methods, including eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGB), Random Forest (RF), and Artificial Neural Networks (ANN), were developed and interpreted using SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP). The sensitivity analysis indicates that the fraction of recovered heat (R) is the most influential input variable affecting the output variables. An increase in R reduces the required capacities of PV, WT, and BES, while increasing the energy self-consumption rate (E<sub>SCR</sub>) and the net present value (NPV), and simultaneously decreasing the investment cost (INV). The effects of the number of household members (NM), electricity purchase price (P<sub>e</sub>), and electricity demand multiplier (M<sub>ed</sub>) are generally moderate. In contrast, the electricity selling price multiplier (M<sub>Pes</sub>) shows only a marginal impact, which results from the high level of self-consumption in the analyzed energy systems. Overall, the results demonstrate that integrating DWHR with renewable energy systems can significantly enhance building energy efficiency and reduce operating costs. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
