	abstract = {High renewable penetration, increased power-electronics interfacing, and rapidly varying demand push modern power grids toward tighter operational margins. Operators must solve a sequence of decisions - voltage and reactive power control, contingency-aware dispatch, and (in some regions) topology switching - under nonconvex AC physics and hard safety constraints. This paper presents a quantitative, simulation-driven research study of a practical machine-learning (ML) pipeline for grid management that combines: (i) a graph neural network (GNN) surrogate to warm-start AC optimal power flow (ACOPF) solvers; (ii) a safe reinforcement learning (RL) corrective controller for fast voltage recovery; and (iii) a lightweight trust filter for retrieved operational procedures. Using a pandapower-based AC backend and IEEE-style benchmark networks, we report that GNN warm starts can reduce per-scenario solve time substantially while retaining solver feasibility, and that a barrier-based safety layer can cut voltage-violation probability relative to unconstrained RL while preserving near-optimal operational cost. The results support a hybrid deployment strategy that keeps conventional solvers in the loop, uses ML for speed, and makes safety constraints first-class citizens. © 2026 IEEE.},
