	abstract = {Accurate electricity load forecasting is essential for ensuring power grid stability and supporting efficient energy planning. This study presents a comparative analysis of several deep learning models—including Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU), Recurrent Neural Network (RNN), Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN), and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)—alongside machine learning models such as CatBoost, XGBoost, and LightGBM. These models were evaluated across six diverse hourly load consumption datasets from Portugal, Nova Scotia, France, Italy, Denmark, and Spain, covering one-year and three-year periods. Randomized Grid Search was used to fine-tune model hyperparameters. The results show that model performance varies by region and dataset duration. LightGBM consistently outperformed other models, achieving the best performance on the Spain dataset with an nRMSE of 2.31 %, MAE of 397.8, and R² of 0.98 for the one-year dataset, and an improved nRMSE of 1.3 % for the three-year dataset. XGBoost also performed well with an nRMSE of 3.09 % and R² of 0.98 on the Italy dataset. In contrast, LSTM showed weaker results with an nRMSE of 9.53 % and R² of 0.60 on the Portugal dataset. Among deep models, RNN stood out slightly, achieving an nRMSE of 4.8 % and R² of 0.90 on the Spain dataset. Overall, gradient boosting models proved to be more robust and accurate for short-term load forecasting tasks. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.},
