	abstract = {Accurate modeling of photovoltaic modules is essential for performance prediction and optimization in solar energy systems. However, reliable estimation of internal resistances under practical operating conditions remains a significant challenge, as most existing extraction methods are validated using simulated data or standard test conditions. This limitation motivates the need for a systematic and comparative evaluation of internal resistance extraction techniques using real-world photovoltaic data to ensure accurate and application-oriented PV modeling. Among various model parameters, the internal resistances R <sub>s </sub> and R <sub>sh </sub> play a critical role in determining the output efficiency of PV panels. This study presents a comparative analysis of internal resistance estimation using four parameter extraction methods: the Villalva analytical method, the Newton-Raphson iterative method, Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO), and MATLAB-based curve fitting. Five commercial PV modules of varying specifications and technologies (mono-crystalline and poly-crystalline) were selected from different manufacturers. Real-world testing was conducted using a Chroma 62050H programmable DC power supply, configured as a solar array emulator. The emulator was governed by I-V data generated using the Sandia National Laboratories Excel tool, enabling accurate emulation of PV behavior under varying irradiance and temperature. The results exhibit significant notable deviation in estimated internal resistances depending on both the extraction method and the PV model. Among the evaluated techniques, the Villalva method given the fastest computation time with acceptable accuracy, making it suitable for embedded or real-time systems. Newton-Raphson exhibited superior convergence behavior, requiring fewer iterations, whereas PSO provided more accurate resistance values but incurred the highest computational cost. The curve-fitting approach achieved a trade-off betw