	abstract = {Accurate Electricity Price Forecasting (EPF) is essential not only for power system planning but also for advancing consumer technologies and enabling smart home systems to operate more efficiently by anticipating price fluctuations. However, the growing integration of renewable energy sources poses significant challenges for EPF, including high price volatility, frequent price spikes, negative pricing, and complex dependencies within grid data. To address these challenges, we propose a hybrid deep learning CNN-LSTM model designed to improve Short-Term Price Forecasting (STPF) in renewable-rich markets. Our ensemble approach captures intricate temporal and spatial patterns in grid data and improves forecast accuracy. The proposed model was evaluated using electricity price data from the South Australian market, a region known for its high price volatility. We conducted a comparative analysis between our model, the AEMO forecasting model and several widely-used machine learning benchmarks, including SVR, Random Forest, and XGBoost. Our model demonstrated superior performance, achieving a 53% reduction in Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) relative to the AEMO model and a 23% lower Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) than XGBoost. Furthermore, it achieved the highest accuracy in detecting both price spikes and negative prices, offering valuable insights for informed decision-making in the energy sector.  © 2025 IEEE.},
