	abstract = {Annual material demand forecasting for power grids is a critical step in achieving lean material management and ensuring stable grid operation. However, traditional forecasting methods struggle to effectively integrate multi-source heterogeneous data and capture its complex nonlinear characteristics, resulting in limited prediction accuracy and practicality. To address this, this paper proposes an annual material demand forecasting model based on an improved convolutional neural network. First, seven quantifiable annual influencing factors are selected from multiple dimensionsincluding planned investments, equipment status, and external environments-to establish a structured feature input system. Subsequently, a one-dimensional convolutional neural network (MRCNN) is designed, integrating a multiscale parallel convolutional neural networkm with residual connection mechanisms to enhance the extraction and fusion of temporal features and cross-factor interactions. Simulation results using a provincial power grid enterprise's 10 kV oil-immersed transformers demonstrate that the proposed model achieves optimal performance on both training and testing datasets. Its fitting and prediction errors are significantly lower than those of comparison models such as multiple linear regression, support vector regression, random forests, and gated recurrent units (GRU), validating the model's effectiveness in improving prediction accuracy and generalization capabilities. The research findings in this paper provide an effective tool for power grid enterprises to achieve data-driven intelligent forecasting of annual material requirements. © 2026 IEEE.},
