	doi = {10.1016/j.ecmx.2026.101607},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031394370?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Reliable solar radiation estimation is vital for sustainable energy planning in data‑scarce regions such as Ethiopia. This study comparatively evaluated five predictive models − Generalized Regression Neural Network (GRNN), Support Vector Regression (SVR), modified Ångström‑Prescott Solar Duration Fraction (SDF), Hourly Data Mining (HDM), and the hybrid physical‑data model (Solpy) − using measured solar radiation and sunshine duration data from four sites covering 2011–2015 and 2018–2019. Model accuracy was analyzed using correlation coefficient (R), root mean square error (RMSE), and mean absolute error (MAE). The GRNN and SVR models, trained on hourly data, demonstrated superior predictive accuracy for daily global horizontal radiation (average = 6.23 kWh/m<sup>2</sup>·day) compared with empirical and hybrid techniques. GRNN achieved the best performance (RMSE = 0.23 kWh/m<sup>2</sup>‑day, MAE = 0.18 kWh/m<sup>2</sup>‑day, R = 0.95), followed by SVR, Solpy, and linear SDF, with respective RMSE percentage errors of 3.7%, 4%, 4%, and 9.5%. Validation using the independent 2011–2015 dataset confirmed model robustness, while Solpy maintained cross‑location accuracy with errors between 6.1% and 6.3% and closely matched TRNSYS Type 16 simulations for 15°-tilted surfaces (average deviation = 6%). Hourly estimations exhibited greater variability (13–16% error) due to cloud dynamics and short-term diffuse radiation fluctuations. Overall, GRNN, SVR, linear SDF, HDM, and Solpy demonstrate strong potential for solar energy assessment in Ethiopia's semi‑arid regions. Notably, GRNN and Solpy deliver highly accurate yet data-efficient predictions, making them suitable for areas with incomplete sunshine records. These models outperform previously reported ANN and empirical methods, enabling more reliable solar resource mapping for renewable energy development. © 2026},
