	doi = {10.1016/j.compeleceng.2026.111201},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036564169?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Accurate forecasting of electric vehicle (EV) charging demand is essential for managing smart grid operations, optimizing infrastructure planning, and ensuring the effective integration of renewable energy sources. However, existing forecasting models often face key limitations, including limited temporal resolution, low adaptability to real-world data variability, and poor sensitivity to external environmental factors. Moreover, prevalent point forecasts fail to quantify the uncertainty required for robust grid management. To address these challenges, this study proposes a novel forecasting framework based on the Temporal Fusion Transformer (TFT), a deep learning architecture capable of modeling complex temporal patterns and integrating multiple covariates. The TFT is chosen for its built-in multi-horizon forecasting and interpretability. The main objective is to develop a robust, interpretable, and high-accuracy model for predicting EV charging loads on both hourly and daily timescales with multi-horizon settings of 1–24 h and 1–7 days. The objective includes generating conditional probabilistic forecasts by training on quantile loss to enable risk assessment. The proposed TFT model incorporates a rich set of environmental and temporal features (e.g., temperature, rainfall, solar radiation, time-of-day, and calendar variables), supports multi-horizon prediction, and leverages attention mechanisms to highlight influential features (feature attribution via the TFT variable-selection and attention layers). Compared to conventional methods including Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), and Seasonal Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (SARIMA), as well as strong statistical baselines (persistence and seasonal naïve), the TFT model demonstrates superior performance across a comprehensive set of evaluation metrics, including Mean Absolute Error (MAE), Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), Mean