	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105019091982?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The growing energy demand in a near-zero carbon rural industrial park in China's cold climate zones is characterized by high seasonal heating and cooling requirements, heavy reliance on fossil fuels, and limited penetration of renewable energy. This study proposes a multi-energy complementary combined cooling, heating, and power system integrating parabolic trough collectors, photovoltaic panels, wind turbines, biogas generators, ground source heat pumps, gas turbines, absorption chillers, and organic Rankine cycles to maximize renewable energy utilization and reduce environmental impacts. A hybrid approach integrating TRNSYS dynamic simulation and response surface methodology is employed to evaluate the nonlinear interactions among design variables and optimize system performance across four dimensions: primary energy ratio, exergy efficiency, net present value, and pollutant emission reductions. Results show that photovoltaic panels capacity has the greatest influence on economic and environmental outcomes, with optimal configurations achieving a net present value of 265,022 $, annual CO<sub>2</sub> reduction of 323 ton, and life cycle cost savings of up to 210 k$. The highest efficiency-oriented configuration attains a primary energy ratio of 0.590 and an exergy efficiency of 0.250, while maintaining a dynamic payback period of 6–7 years. These findings demonstrate that a balanced configuration of renewable resources can minimize grid dependence, improve sustainability, and ensure favorable economic returns. The proposed framework provides practical guidance for the planning and deployment of multi-energy systems in rural industrial parks. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
