	doi = {10.1016/j.uncres.2026.100389},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034786598?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {AbstractThis study proposes a novel optimization framework for hybrid renewable energy systems (HRES) based on advanced Machine Learning techniques. The investigated system integrates solar photovoltaic panels, wind turbines, and hydrogen energy system includes hydrogen storage tanks, comprising an electrolyzer, and a fuel cell, with bidirectional grid connectivity to meet the energy demands of a Data Center load profile. The primary objective of this study is making the optimal decision based on the management strategy to minimize the overall system cost while maintaining operational reliability and reducing the environmental impact. To achieve this, three machine learning approaches: Long Short-Term Memory networks (LSTM), Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), and a developed hybrid (LSTM-XGBoost) model are used and compared. These models are dynamically used to forecast the system input data and so select the optimal energy management strategy at hourly intervals (8760 h per year) by evaluating predefined scenarios against a multi-criteria objective function. The objective function considers several key factors and integrates penalty terms. The findings confirm that the proposed hybrid machine learning approach significantly outperforms the baseline models, it achieves a better technical and economic performance metrics than the baseline models (R2 = 0.979, LCOE = 0.158 $/kWh and 27% RMSE reduction), and it improves decision making for the system operation, offering a practical pathway to reduce the system costs while enhancing reliability and environmental performance. © 2026 The Authors. Publishing services by Elsevier B.V. on behalf of KeAi Communications Co. Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/},
