	abstract = {In this study, we present an IoT-enabled, small-scale solar-powered EV charging station with real-time monitoring and control. The system allows remote data collection, which is utilized to estimate the maximum power point (MPP) without requiring a dedicated MPPT unit, replaced by a pulse width modulation (PWM) charge controller, and to predict power generation useful for fault detection and energy planning. Single-axis tracking, driven by an ESP32, light-dependent resistors (LDRs), and a servo motor, maximizes solar capture. Real-time voltage and current data are displayed via Wi-Fi on an OLED and a web interface. Experimental results show that with a 27 W input and a 25 W output, the system achieves an efficiency of 92.6%. Collected current-voltage-temperature data estimate the photovoltaic I-V curve with a 2.5% error in operating current. A machine learning framework predicts solar power output from six environmental features - irradiance, temperature, dew point, humidity, wind speed, and sky condition - using ridge regression (R2 =88%). After feature selection, a support vector regression model achieves R2 = 85.3% using only irradiance and sky condition. Compact, modular, and low-cost, the proposed design offers a practical model for sustainable EV charging integrating renewable energy, IoT, and data-driven intelligence. © 2026 IEEE.},
