	abstract = {Lightning strikes are one of the main causes of faults and outages in power distribution grid, posing a serious threat to the stable operation of power systems. However, traditional lightning disaster risk assessment methods struggle to handle complex factors, making it difficult to meet practical application needs. To address this issue, this paper proposes a lightning risk assessment method based on the Bayesian optimization (BO)-eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) model, where BO is used to select the optimal combination of hyperparameters. Furthermore, to reduce the impact of the limited number of lightning strike samples, a classification model combining K-means Clustering (Kmeans)-Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE) and BO-XGBoost is employed. To validate the performance, different risk assessment models were constructed. Among them, the KMeans-SMOTE-BO-XGBoost model achieved an accuracy of 99.6 %, a recall of 98.1 % and a ROC-AUC score of 0.99, outperforming the other models. After balancing the data with the Kmeans-SMOTE algorithm, the accuracy of the model is improved by 19.8 %. Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP) value analysis demonstrated the impact of different features on the output, providing guidance for optimizing lightning protection in distribution network lines. © 2025},
