	abstract = {With the development of technology, both attack and defense methods have been constantly evolving. Power systems have been equipped with various defense mechanisms to ensure safe operation. However, attackers are also continually designing new combinations of multiple attack methods to launch more powerful, complex, difficult-to-detect, and defend against attacks. As one of the essential safety indicators in power system operation, the sag of overhead transmission lines is also threatened by hybrid cyber attacks. In this study, we propose a hybrid attack strategy that combines DoS and deception attacks to enable attackers to inject false data successfully without being detected, and provide a more realistic attack environment for subsequent sag interval estimation research. To estimate the sag interval, we propose a sag interval estimation model based on Quantile Regression Extreme Gradient Boosting (QRXGBoost) to capture the uncertainty in the estimation values and provide more accurate estimation intervals under hybrid cyber attacks. Additionally, we introduce a Mixed Parameter Optimization (MPO) method combining random search and Bayesian optimization to optimize model parameters and improve parameter search efficiency. This method identifies superior parameter combinations, further improving the performance of the sag interval estimation model under hybrid cyber attacks. We verify the effectiveness of the proposed QRXGBoost model through comparative analysis with three improved models and validate the robustness of the proposed model against attack data through experiments considering four scenarios of training and testing with and without attacks. Our results demonstrate the efficacy of our proposed approach in transmission line sag interval estimation under the threat of hybrid cyber attacks. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.},
