	abstract = {Wind energy systems (WES) enhance power quality by reducing harmonic distortion along with improving voltage regulation. However, it makes the system's design and control challenging. In order to enhance power quality in WES using a Quasi-Z-Source Inverter with Fractional Order Proportional Integral Derivative control (FOPID), this research suggests the FOPID-SAPO method, which combines Surrogate-Assisted Pelican Optimization (SAPO) with Covariance Matrix Adaptation (CMA). The main objective of the proposed approach is to improve the system's performance and PQ while also increasing the WES's efficiency and reliability. The SAPO enhances the gains of the FOPID controller, while the CMA predicts the control signal of the inverter. The proposed method performs better than existing techniques like Aquila Optimizer (AO), Slime Mould Algorithm (SMA), and Grey Wolf Optimizer (GWO) when tested and compared using the MATLAB platform with an efficiency of 92% and a voltage THD of 21.82%, the proposed technique exhibits both lower voltage THD and higher efficiency when compared to the existing method. Furthermore, compared to current methods, the peak time is 0.15 seconds, and the settling time is 0.27 seconds. The proposed SAPO-CMA approach significantly increases power quality and efficiency in WES with QZSI integrated FOPID control, outperforming existing optimization techniques. © 2026 IEEE.},
