	abstract = {The integration of inverter-based renewable energy sources is progressively displacing conventional synchronous generators, leading to critically low system inertia and increased vulnerability to frequency instability in modern power systems. To address this, virtual inertia emulation has emerged as a vital solution for this issue. This paper specifically leverages the rapid response capability of Electric Vehicle (EV) aggregators, which can act as large-scale, distributed resources to generate or absorb active power almost instantaneously during system imbalances. In this context, this paper proposes a novel load frequency control framework designed to maximize the effectiveness of this virtual inertia. The new cascaded control structure includes a fractional-order controller combining Tilt-Integral-Derivative and Tilt-Derivative elements (the FOTID-FOTD controller) for superior load frequency regulation. Moreover, to overcome the critical challenge of tuning this complex controller, a newly enhanced metaheuristic optimizer, the Enhanced Slime Mould Algorithm (ESMA), has been developed. The ESMA incorporates Levy flight and chaotic maps into the standard Slime Mould Algorithm to ensure simultaneous and global optimization of all controller parameters, effectively avoiding local minima. Comprehensive simulation studies on a two-area power system demonstrate that the co-designed FOTID-FOTD controller and ESMA optimizer pair significantly enhances frequency stability and dynamic performance, effectively harnessing EV aggregation to restore resilience in low-inertia power grids. © 2013 IEEE.},
