	abstract = {Accurate short-term electric vehicle (EV) load forecasting is critical for grid stability and demand response management amid rapidly increasing EV penetration. However, existing statistical, ensemble, and deep learning models often rely on implicit sequence learning and struggle to jointly capture multi-scale temporal structure, nonlinear interactions, and cross-dataset heterogeneity in EV charging demand. This paper proposes a decomposition-driven hybrid forecasting framework that integrates Seasonal-Trend decomposition using Loess (STL) with component-specific learning and stacked ensemble fusion. The EV load signal is decomposed into trend, seasonal, and residual components, each modeled using an algorithm aligned with its statistical characteristics. Component-level predictions are then fused through a Gradient Boosting Regressor meta-learner to enhance robustness and generalization. Experimental evaluation on four real-world EV charging datasets (ACN, Palo Alto, Perth, and Paris) demonstrates consistent performance gains over representative statistical, ensemble, and deep learning baselines. On the ACN dataset, the proposed framework achieves MAE 0.614, RMSE 0.797, and MAPE 12.7%, corresponding to more than 90% and 88% reductions in RMSE and MAPE, respectively, relative to the strongest benchmark. These results highlight the effectiveness and scalability of the proposed approach for practical smart grid deployment. © 2010-2012 IEEE.},
