	abstract = {Hybrid Zn-ion/air batteries have recently emerged as promising candidates for combining the specific capacity of Zn–ion batteries with the high voltage of Zn–air systems, yet their coupled reaction mechanism remains poorly understood. Here, a novel integrated modeling framework was established by combining an experimentally validated physics-based model—governed by mass and charge transport equations coupled with Butler-Volmer kinetics—with intelligent machine learning algorithms to assess the performance of hybrid zinc batteries. The physics-based numerical model was developed and utilized to generate a comprehensive dataset spanning 405 operating scenarios. This dataset trained two advanced algorithms—Bayesian-optimized Least Squares Support Vector Machine (BO-LSSVM) and Random Forest (BO-RF)—to predict two critical battery metrics: the specific capacity of the Zn–ion and the discharge voltage of the Zn–air regions. Sensitivity analysis identified key design criteria, revealing that the active material load and inlet oxygen mole fraction strongly enhance the performance of both areas. Additionally, cathode thickness, electrolyte concentration, current density, and PTFE coating time require precise tuning. The results also indicated that balancing ion transport in the Zn-ion region with oxygen reduction kinetics in the Zn-air side is essential for achieving uniform potential distribution and minimizing internal resistance losses. The BO-LSSVM model demonstrated high accuracy, with an R<sup>2</sup> value of 0.980 for estimating the specific capacities of the Zn-ion region. In contrast, the BO-RF model excelled in predicting the discharge voltages of the Zn-air region with an R<sup>2</sup> of 0.998. Collectively, the developed framework provides a battery-level understanding of how design parameters correlate into measurable electrochemical performance indicators. This integrated strategy can guide material selection and electrode architecture optimizati