	abstract = {High penetration of photovoltaic (PV) generation introduces strong intermittency and uncertainty into power systems, making accurate short-term PV power forecasting essential for secure operation and economic dispatch. Conventional statistical models and single machine-learning predictors often struggle to capture the nonlinear relationships among historical power, meteorological variables, and numerical weather prediction (NWP) outputs, especially under rapidly changing cloud conditions. This paper proposes a physics-informed stacking ensemble framework for short-term PV power forecasting at a grid-connected plant. A multi-source feature set integrates historical PV power, on-site meteorological measurements, NWP-derived variables, and time-solar-geometry descriptors, with normalization by installed capacity or clear-sky irradiance. Several heterogeneous base regressors, including Random Forest, gradient boosting, support vector regression, and a lightweight neural network, are trained using a sliding-window scheme, and their out-of-fold predictions are combined by a meta-learner to improve accuracy. A physics-informed post-processing layer enforces non-negativity, capacity limits, and night-time behavior. Experiments on real PV power and weather data demonstrate that the proposed method consistently outperforms persistence, individual base models, and simple averaging ensembles in terms of root-mean-square error (RMSE), mean absolute error (MAE), mean absolute percentage error (MAPE), and R<sup>2.</sup> Improved tracking of ramps and reduced forecast errors highlight the value of the approach for reserve scheduling, intra-day dispatch, and storage management in PV-integrated power systems. © 2026 SPIE.},
