	abstract = {With the advancement of power technologies in the modern era, electric load forecasting has become increasingly crucial in domains including power grid layout optimization, electricity consumption, and power transmission. Precise electric load forecasting can substantially enhance multiple facets of the power industry, spanning economic efficiency, capital investment, energy utilization, and human resource allocation. This study employs an random forest regression model for accurate power load forecasting, using a time-series dataset split into a training set (≤ 2018) and a test set (2019). Features include historical load, temperature (lowtemp, hightemp), and temporal indicators (hour, month, etc.), with future load (loady) as the target. Correlation analysis shows high-order polynomial (e.g., hightemp 3, r=0.4463) and cross-term temperature features (e.g., lowtemphightemp 2, r=0.4433) better capture load relationships than single temperature variables. Temporal analysis reveals load peaks in summer/winter and at 17-19 o'clock daily, with minimal weekday impact. Model experiments demonstrate optimal performance with all features, excluding time features causes a significant R<sup>2</sup> drop. Hyperparameter tuning finds best results with n_estimators =100/300/500, min_samples_leaf =1/5, and max_features =1.0. This paper's contribution is twofold: first, it demonstrates that systematic feature engineering, particularly with high-order temperature interactions, is crucial for capturing the non-linear drivers of electricity demand. Second, through a rigorous comparative analysis, we validate that the Random Forest framework not only surpasses conventional statistical models but also outperforms other prevalent machine learning benchmarks (Decision Tree, MLP, AdaBoost) in forecasting accuracy. Prediction results show close alignment between predicted and actual load curves, with error density peaking around 0. The model exhibits strong fitting, generaliza