	abstract = {As climate change and extreme weather events increasingly challenge energy systems, existing solar irradiance prediction models face limitations in accuracy and stability under abnormal conditions, restricting the efficiency of energy scheduling and resource planning. This study focuses on improving the accuracy and robustness of daily solar irradiance predictions by investigating the role of meteorological, air pollution, and temporal factors influencing daily global horizontal irradiance (GHI) and constructing a near-real-time prediction model with enhanced stability and generalization. By leveraging the Typical Meteorological Year (TMY) framework to capture long-term trends and applying ensemble learning techniques to model short-term variations, this study ensures both stability and adaptability in solar irradiance predictions. To achieve this goal, Himawari-8 and MTSAT-2 satellite data were integrated with ground-based meteorological observations and air pollution factors. A TMY was constructed using the Sandia method to ensure data representativeness and stability. Stacking ensemble learning techniques were employed to combine base models, including Random Forest (RF), eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP), and Extra Trees (ET), enabling feature importance analysis and the construction of an accurate prediction model. The results demonstrate that the Stacking model significantly outperforms individual models, achieving an R<sup>2</sup> of 0.95, with RMSE and MAE reduced to 0.0224 kWh/m<sup>2/hour</sup> and 0.0164 kWh/m<sup>2/</sup> hour, respectively. Feature importance analysis highlights daily maximum temperature and precipitation as critical influencing factors, while air pollution factors, though less weighted, exhibit notable indirect impacts under future scenarios of high pollution or sustained warming. This study illustrates that ensemble learning effectively enhances the accuracy and stability of solar irradianc