	abstract = {As a new practical and economical solution to the aging problem of overhead line (OHL) assets, the technical policies of most power grid companies in the world experienced a gradual transition from scheduled preventive maintenance to a risk-based approach in asset management. Although the accumulation of contamination is predictable to a certain degree, and various methods have been proposed to assess the risk of insulator flashover, it remains significant potential to improve these methods and to develop more reliable and efficient strategies for insulator maintenance planning. This paper presents a novel machine learning (ML) based method for estimating the flashover probability of the cup-and-pin glass insulator string. Utilizing the Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) supervised ML model, the method incorporates the measurement results of leakage current (LC) features and applied voltage as the input. The proposed method can be divided into two stages. The first stage contains a classification model which achieves over 98% F1-score accuracy in estimating humidity level as a binary representation (dry or wet). In the second stage, the regression model predicts the critical flashover voltage (U<sub>50%</sub>) for various OHL insulator designs across different voltage levels. Results indicate that by selecting the optimal input features, the error of the flashover prediction can be minimized to around 1.16% according to the root mean square error (RMSE) metric. Furthermore, the proposed method is able to accurately determine the dielectric strength of the insulator strings and instruct asset management engineers to take appropriate actions. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
