	abstract = {The electricity load has a relatively long history among forecasting domains, as it provides basis for many applications. Long-term (month or year-ahead) forecasting is essential for network development; week-ahead prognosis is used for planning maintenance work on power lines; day-ahead forecasts are effective input for market price calculations; and intraday or ultra short-term predictions support imbalance forecasting. This paper presents a discussion and methodology for day-ahead total load forecasting in the Hungarian bidding zone based on to the data description of ENTSO-e. Since the price flexibility of electricity demand in the short term tends to be low, the idea is that total load should be quite accurately predictable in a top-down approach based on calendar variables like date types, time information, weather forecasts and short-past trends. However, this only stands if the forecast and the fact data is correctly calculated. Currently, only 18 countries provide continuous and good quality data about their load on the ENTSO-e Transparency Platform. In this paper we provide insight on an updated methodology of data publication and forecasting in Hungary, considering the effect of small-scale unmeasured photovoltaic systems based on a recent developed estimation methodology of the Hungarian Transmission System Operator. In this study a blended machine learning approach is proposed using Extreme Gradient Boosting and Light Gradient boosting algorithms trained with different retrospect and predictor combinations. The best individual model results in a 3.61 % nRMSE, which is further improved to 3.29% with model blending and finding the best combination of models for different date types. This result is in the top five among the European countries in terms of 15-minutes day-ahead total load forecasting. The proposed methodology corrects some errors of the current data publication and provides a significantly more accurate forecast than the current 