	abstract = {Photovoltaic (PV) power forecasting is essential for optimizing renewable energy systems, yet remains challenging due to the dynamic interplay of environmental and temporal factors. Traditional approaches, typically based on single models or conventional ensembles, often fall short in capturing complex nonlinear dependencies and generalizing across diverse operational conditions. In this work, a robust stacking ensemble framework is proposed that integrates three hybrid base models - eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LightGBM), and TabNet - combined via Ridge regression as the meta-learner. The framework employs multilevel feature engineering, including temporal, lag-based, rolling statistical, and interaction features, to capture rich time-series dynamics. Hyperparameters are efficiently optimized using Optuna, enhancing training efficiency and model performance. Experiments conducted on a real-world PV dataset from the National Renewable Energy Laboratory (NREL) CIS-1 system (OEDI, Golden, Colorado, 2020-2022) show that the proposed model achieves a root mean square error (RMSE) of 16.19, a mean absolute error (MAE) of 6.50, and a coefficient of determination (R<sup>2</sup>) of 0.9967, significantly outperforming single-model baselines. This interpretable and scalable forecasting framework demonstrates strong potential for deployment in intelligent renewable energy management and smart grid optimization. © PIERS-FALL 2025.All rights reserved.},
