	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032537099?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {As power system reforms intensify globally, wind generation integration has achieved unprecedented scale. The inherent variability of its output presents acute stability challenges to grid operations. Analyses by international energy research institutions demonstrate that in grids where wind penetration surpasses 20 % of total capacity, each 1 % reduction in forecast error translates to annual operational cost savings of € 1. 2 million (based on benchmark testing of a typical 1, 0 0 0 - M W} power grid architecture). However, conventional support vector regression (SVR) models widely adopted in current forecasting systems exhibit limitations due to their dependency on manual parameter tuning, making hyperparameter adaptation difficult for complex wind power fluctuations. It substantially restricts improvements in prediction accuracy and cost control potential. To address this issue, this paper proposes an adaptive particle swarm optimization (APSO)-based SVR hyperparameter optimization model. The algorithm employs dynamic inertia weight adjustment mechanisms to balance global exploration and local exploitation capabilities, while introducing a learning factor optimization strategy driven by population diversity to effectively suppress premature convergence phenomena. Simulation analyses conducted using real measurement data from a German Amprion Energy Corporation wind farm demonstrate significant performance improvements: Compared with traditional SVR models, the APSO-SVR model achieves reductions in root mean square error (RMSE) and mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) of 32.19% and 16.49% respectively. The coefficient of determination (R2) is enhanced to 0.969, verifying strong adaptability to non-stationary wind power sequences. This research provides an extendable technical pathway for enhancing prediction accuracy under high renewable energy penetration scenarios, offering practical engineering value for power system operation and management. © 2