	abstract = {Electricity load forecasting at the household level is an important research direction in modern power systems, driven by the demand for efficient energy management and cost reduction. Accurate daily forecasts help households optimize electricity usage and budgeting under tiered tariff structures, while also enhancing grid reliability and supporting national dispatch centers. However, traditional statistical and similarity-based methods often fail to capture the nonlinear and non-stationary nature of residential consumption, leading to significant errors. To address this issue, we propose a hybrid model that integrates Linear Regression and Extreme Gradient Boosting. The Linear Regression component captures global trends and seasonal effects, whereas Extreme Gradient Boosting learns from residuals to model complex nonlinear relationships and irregular usage patterns. The model is trained and validated on real household data, with performance evaluated using Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) and Root Mean Square Error (RMSE). Experimental results show that the proposed hybrid approach reduces MAPE from 37.9% with standalone Linear Regression to 12.26% and achieves notable improvements in RMSE as well. These findings demonstrate the effectiveness of combining linear and boosting methods for practical household load forecasting, with potential applications in end-user energy awareness, utility planning, and deployment on low-power devices for real-time management.  © 2025 IEEE.},
