	abstract = {To address the problem that PV (Photovoltaic) power generation is significantly affected by meteorological factors, while traditional prediction models suffer from insufficient accuracy and robustness, this study proposes a short-term power prediction method integrating physical mechanisms and statistical learning. Firstly, a physical model is constructed based on PV conversion principles to quantify the impact of core meteorological factors such as irradiance and temperature, generating a theoretical power benchmark with NWP (Numerical Weather Prediction) data as input. Subsequently, taking the outputs of the physical model as core features, combined with Feature Engineering and multi-stage iterative optimization, establishing a Random Forest statistical model to dynamically correct physical deviations. Comparative experiments with the Physical Model, GBDT (Gradient Boosting Decision Tree), and LR(Linear Regression) show that the RF (Random Forest) model performs the best. This model integrates physical interpretability and data adaptability, effectively avoiding the shortcomings of pure physical models and pure data-driven models. It provides a reliable technical path for PV power station power prediction and optimal power grid dispatching. © 2025 IEEE.},
