	abstract = {This study investigates enhancing energy efficiency and resource recovery, including heat recovery and biofuel production, through mathematical modelling of a full-scale wastewater treatment plant (WWTP). By implementing a continuous Modified Ludzack-Ettinger (A02A-MLE) process to replace the intermittent MLE process in plant, the study achieved a 50 % reduction in energy use and GHG emissions while improving total nitrogen (TN) removal and overall system performance. The influence of operational parameters such as pH, dissolved oxygen (DO) concentration, C/N ratio, hydraulic retention time (HRT), influent rate (Q<sub>inf</sub>), and return activated sludge (RAS) was analysed for their impact on ammonia, TN, and total phosphorus (TP) removal, energy costs, and GHG emissions across operational temperatures (10<sup>C</sup>, 20<sup>C</sup> 30<sup>C</sup>, and 40<sup>C</sup>). Using statistical methods like the Taguchi method and Response Surface Methodology, the optimal scenario achieved nearly 100 % ammonia removal, effective TN and TP removal, operational costs of 42.42 kW/d, and GHG emissions of 1100 tCO₂e/yr under conditions of DO at 0.5 mg/L, Q<sub>inf</sub> of 5000 m<sup>3</sup>/d, HRT of 2 days, and a C/N ratio of 15. The optimized system was further evaluated for energy recovery potential, with heat recovery calculated at 242 kW per 1<sup>°C</sup> for a Q<sub>inf</sub> of 5000 m<sup>3</sup>/d at temperatures exceeding 20 °<sup>C</sup>. Biogas production through anaerobic digestion was also analysed under varying operational temperatures, including mesophilic (35<sup>C</sup>) and thermophilic (50<sup>°</sup><sup>C</sup>) conditions. Results demonstrate that the A02A-MLE process enhances TN removal efficiency and improves WWTP sustainability by integrating renewable energy systems, underscoring its economic and environmental benefits for full-scale applications. © 2025 The Authors},
