	abstract = {Determining the degradation of efficiency in synchronous generators is crucial to power system maintenance to ensure the availability of power and minimise lifecycle costs. This paper proposes an AI-based model that forecasts efficiency loss based on regularly measured operational variables in order to determine efficiency loss at an early stage and decide on maintenance scheduling. The preprocessing of a harmonized dataset that is composed of load, vibration, temperature, and operating hours are processed through imputation, outlier control, and normalization. We compare the performance of the three models (Random Forest Regressor, multilayer perceptron (MLP) and support vector regression with an RBF kernel) on an 80/20 split, 10-fold cross-validation, and hyperparameters that are grid-searched. Findings indicate that the suggested method has continuously obtained lower mean squared error and greater R<sup>2</sup> than baseline options, with the MLP having the most suitable and consistent forecasts on test cases. The analysis also indicates how the operation variables impact on predictive performance in support of interpretable decision-making. The method allows scheduling condition-based maintenance and enhancing the utilization of assets by giving credible predictions of efficiency degradation in the short term. The model is extremely model-agnostic and can be easily expanded to larger data regimes and generators. © 2025 IEEE.},
