	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105021976944?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Forecasting wind speed accurately is vital for improving turbine efficiency, planning energy supply and ensuring stable grid operation. However, capturing the unpredictable nature of wind, shaped by varied geographical and climatic conditions, remains a challenge. Majority of conventional techniques are primarily based on point predictions and lack usefulness in applications where uncertainty is the core function, for example, energy trading and short-term dispatching. In this paper, we present a new hybrid ensemble prediction system that utilizes Generalized Likelihood Uncertainty Estimation (GLUE) method to address this gap. The ensemble combines five predictive models ARIMA, Random Forest, XGBoost, LSTM and CNN-LSTM and assigns probabilistic weights based on their mean squared errors, enabling both accurate forecasting and uncertainty quantification. Evaluated the model using a large-scale dataset of over 3.3 million records from wind turbines, which included parameters such as wind speed, direction, ambient temperature, and corresponding power output. GLUE-based ensemble achieved an RMSE of 0.642, performing more consistently than the individual models. Although Random Forest and XGBoost achieved slightly lower RMSE values individually, the GLUE ensemble provides the advantage of predictive uncertainty quantification, which is necessary for operational energy systems to make risk-aware decisions. The framework also provides 95% Prediction Intervals (PI) that encompass the majority of actual observations, making it suitable for operational deployment in risk-aware energy systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
