	abstract = {The operational efficiency and scalability of alkaline water electrolysis (AWE) systems for green hydrogen production face significant challenges because of electrode and membrane component longevity. A research study introduces an integrated machine learning system that predicts electrode and membrane material degradation patterns under different operational conditions when powered by solar photovoltaic (PV) energy sources. A laboratory-scale AWE system consisted of nickel-coated stainless-steel mesh as the cathode and Ni-Fe layered double hydroxide (LDH) coated electrodes as the anode. Operational datasets comprising current density, voltage, temperature, electrolyte concentration, and operating time were collected and supplemented with simulated data. These were used to train Random Forest, Support Vector Regression, and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) models. The LSTM achieved the best performance R2 = 0.92, RMSE = 0.038 V, MAE = 0.029 V), followed by Random Forest (R2=0.89) and SVR (R2=0.85). The LSTM model achieved exceptional temporal prediction accuracy by forecasting degradation trends with more than 95% precision. The analysis of feature importance showed that current density together with operating temperature play the most significant roles in material degradation processes. The AI-based predictive tool enables both fault detection at an early stage and proactive system maintenance and optimization for solar-integrated electrolysis units. The proposed approach enables the creation of intelligent self-adaptive green hydrogen systems which align with smart energy systems and AI applications in engineering initiatives. © 2025 IEEE.},
