	abstract = {Reliable fault detection and classification in electrical transmission systems is essential for maintaining system stability, safety, and performance. This paper presents an intelligent fault diagnosis framework employing a Random Forest (RF) classifier optimized using Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) for the classification of faults in high-voltage transmission lines. The proposed model is designed to classify a total of 12 classes, comprising 11 distinct fault types and one healthy (no-fault) condition. The Random Forest algorithm, known for its robustness and ensemble learning capabilities, is enhanced by optimizing its most critical hyper parameter, the number of decision trees through the PSO met heuristic. PSO efficiently searches the solution space to determine the optimal tree count that maximizes classification accuracy. The system is trained and evaluated using a comprehensive dataset generated through simulation studies under varying fault conditions, fault resistances, inception angles, and system configurations. Experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of the PSO-optimized RF model, achieving an outstanding classification accuracy of 99.6914%, confirming its ability to accurately distinguish between complex fault scenarios. © 2025 IEEE.},
