	abstract = {High-voltage circuit breakers have a significant impact on power grid stability. Mechanical property testing is required before commissioning. Failure to meet these requirements requires component disassembly and adjustment of basic parameters. To address the inefficiency of manual adjustment of basic parameters and the high cost of repeated component disassembly, this paper proposes an intelligent prediction and decision-making method for high-voltage circuit breaker mechanical properties based on a random forest algorithm. First, an LW25-126 SF6 circuit breaker is selected as the research object, and a dataset containing key basic parameters and key mechanical property parameters is constructed. The dataset is augmented using the generate_realistic_data routine and SMOTE oversampling to balance the proportion of positive and negative samples. Then, a grid search and k-fold cross-validation optimization method are used to predict key mechanical parameters based on the random forest model. Finally, the predicted results are compared with technical specifications in a rule engine. The predicted results are first output as a diagnosis of the device status, and then quantitative adjustment recommendations for the basic parameters are generated, forming a closed-loop decision-making process. Results show that the proposed method achieves a diagnostic accuracy of 87.5% and can provide effective basic parameter adjustment recommendations. These findings can provide a reference for the overhaul process of high-voltage circuit breakers. © 2025 IEEE.},
