	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105019737586?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This chapter systematically explores methods for modeling and forecasting urban electric vehicle (EV) charging loads. First, a medium-term to long-term ownership forecast model is developed using an improved Bass diffusion model. This model integrates factors such as policy incentives (subsidies and license plate discounts), technological competition (fuel vehicle substitution), and market capacity. It also incorporates a Weibull distribution to simulate vehicle scrappage rates. Using Shanghai as a case study, the model predicts that by 2030, EVs will dominate the private car market, and quantifies the impact of uncertainties related to policy and environmental parameters. Second, the chapter proposes a spatiotemporal forecasting method for wide-area charging loads based on travel chain theory and Monte Carlo simulation. By categorizing travel patterns (e.g., "home-work-home" chains) and analyzing behavioral statistical features using National Household Travel Survey data, the method simulates charging demand distribution by incorporating points of interest. A charging heatmap for Shanghai is generated, revealing load aggregation patterns, particularly in central urban areas. Finally, for the optimization of charging station operations, a data-driven ultra-short-term load forecasting framework is introduced, breaking the limitations of traditional linear models. Machine learning techniques (such as neural networks and random forests) are employed to address the challenges of load randomness and spatiotemporal heterogeneity. The chapter integrates policy analysis, behavioral modeling, and data science methods to provide theoretical support for charging facility planning and enhancing the resilience of the power grid. © 2025 Tsinghua University Press Limited, Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
