	abstract = {The integration of electric vehicles (EVs) into modern power grids presents growing challenges related to power loss, load balancing, and grid reliability. This study proposes a machine learning based framework for classifying power loss categories in EV charging networks, aimed at improving operational efficiency and supporting intelligent energy distribution. Four supervised learning models Random Forest, XGBoost, CatBoost, and a Feedforward Neural Network (FNN) are applied to predict the reduced power loss category based on key operational features. The dataset is preprocessed through normalization and correlation based feature selection. Models are evaluated using standard classification metrics, including accuracy, precision, recall, and F 1-score. Among all models, the FNN achieved the highest performance with a classification accuracy of 91.00 percentage, followed by CatBoost and Random Forest. To enhance interpretability, SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values are employed, identifying num power loss as the most influential feature driving model decisions. The analysis of training-validation curves confirms model generalization without overfitting. The proposed approach effectively combines predictive accuracy with model transparency, offering a scalable solution for real time power loss mitigation in EV charging grids. This work contributes to smart grid research by integrating interpretable AI into data driven decision making for sustainable energy infrastructure. © 2025 IEEE.},
