	abstract = {Distribution lines are very prone to icing under extreme weather conditions such as low temperature, rain and snow, which seriously threatens the safety and stable operation of the power supply system. In order to realize intelligent identification and real-time warning of icing risks, this paper proposes an automatic evaluation model for the icing degree of distribution lines based on random forest algorithm. Firstly, a time series data set containing key features such as temperature, humidity, wind speed, and current was constructed through multi-source data collection, and feature selection, standardization, and sample labeling were performed; secondly, combining the ensemble learning advantages of the random forest model, a multi-decision tree joint prediction structure was constructed, and parameter optimization was performed through grid search to improve the generalization ability and discrimination accuracy of the model; subsequently, a robustness verification was carried out in typical meteorological scenarios, and a comparative experiment was conducted with the traditional empirical rule method. The results showed that this model has significant advantages in accuracy, false alarm control, and response efficiency; finally, the real-time and availability of the model in an engineering environment were verified through edge computing platform deployment testing. Research shows that the model has good practicality and engineering promotion value, and can provide data support and intelligent assistance for power grid icing disaster monitoring, early warning and dispatching decisions. © 2025 IEEE.},
