	abstract = {In the face of rising global energy demands and the growing integration of renewable energy sources, smart grid networks require advanced strategies for efficient energy distribution and consumption. This paper proposes a Machine Learning-Based Adaptive Prediction Model designed to optimize energy utilization within smart grid environments. The model employs Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) neural networks to forecast energy demand with high temporal accuracy, leveraging historical consumption data, real-time weather conditions, and dynamic load profiles. To adaptively manage energy resources, a Reinforcement Learning (RL)-based scheduler is integrated to dynamically allocate power from distributed energy resources (DERs), including solar and wind units, based on predicted demand patterns. Additionally, an anomaly detection system using One-Class Support Vector Machines (SVM) ensures grid reliability by identifying abnormal consumption behaviors or potential system faults. The proposed model was evaluated using real-world data from a smart grid testbed encompassing 100 residential and commercial energy consumers, solar irradiance levels, and grid feedback signals. Experimental results demonstrated that the LSTM component achieved a mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) of 3.84% for demand prediction, significantly outperforming traditional models such as ARIMA and Support Vector Regression (SVR). The RL-based optimization reduced peak load by 18.7% and improved overall energy utilization efficiency by 22.5%, while energy procurement costs were lowered by 15.3% over a 30-day test period. Furthermore, the anomaly detection module successfully identified irregularities with an accuracy of 96.1%. These findings affirm that the proposed adaptive machine learning model offers a robust and intelligent solution for real-time prediction, load balancing, and energy optimization in smart grid networks, thereby enhancing sustainability, cost-efficiency, and grid stability.  © 2025 IEEE.},
