	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85214929527?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Increasingly diversified energy resources interactive with power grids have necessitated the deployment of digital real-time systems for performance preview. In this work, a flexible-fidelity model of back-to-back modular multilevel converters with battery energy storage is proposed for hardware emulation of renewable energy integration in real-time and faster-than-real-time. Interfaced by a bidirectional DC–DC converter, the batteries are distributed among submodules to facilitate energy storage management and maintenance. A multi-object control strategy is proposed to maintain a desired amount of power injection into the grid. To ensure fidelity, component-level electromagnetic transient modeling is carried out alongside a topological optimization of the many nodes converter model so that a significant computational burden alleviation is achieved after splitting time-varying elements which subsequently become independent. Meanwhile, the requirement of real-time execution suggests the adoption of field programmable gate arrays owing to its remarkable parallel processing capability. Considering a potential deficiency of hardware resources, a detailed design analysis is conducted for the converter deployment. The integration of wind power is emulated to demonstrate the applicability of the digital twin whose static and dynamic behaviors are justified by detailed analysis and PSCAD®. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
