	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034666703?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Accurate power estimation is a critical requirement in modern VLSI circuit design, directly impacting performance, thermal behavior, and energy efficiency. Traditional simulation-based techniques, such as SPICE-level or gate-level analysis, are highly accurate but computationally intensive and time-consuming, making them unsuitable for early-stage design exploration. This paper proposes a machine learning-based framework for fast and reliable power prediction in digital VLSI circuits using key synthesis-derived parameters such as look up tables (LUTs), input/output count, propagation delay, and operating frequency. A dataset comprising 60 synthesized digital circuits was generated using Xilinx Vivado and Quartus tools, and used to train multiple regression models including Linear Regression, Random Forest, and XGBoost. To prepare the dataset we have used Spartan-3E FPGA (XC3S250E-CP132) and Cyclone 10 GX. The area and timing reports were created using Xilinx Vivado whereas power reports from Quartus tool were analyzed. The XGBoost model outperformed others, achieving an R2 score of 0.9999, a mean absolute error of just 0.1052 and a Mean absolute percentage error of just 0.01. Furthermore, a lightweight web-based interface was developed to allow designers to estimate power in real-time by inputting circuit metrics, thus enabling rapid design-space exploration without requiring hardware simulation. This approach demonstrates the potential of AI to enhance Electronic Design Automation (EDA) workflows and promote power-aware design from the early stages of the VLSI design process achieving 99.99% prediction accuracy with ensemble learning. © 2025 IEEE.},
