	abstract = {Short-Term load forecasting (STLF) is essential for operational scheduling and planning in modern power systems. To address the uncertainty associated with electricity demand, this paper proposes a novel probabilistic forecasting framework that integrates Gradient Boosting Decision Tree (GBDT)-based sister forecasts with ensemble learning strategies. In contrast to prior studies that rely on a single GBDT or simple averaging schemes, the proposed approach develops multiple GBDT learners (LightGBM, XGBoost, and CatBoost) trained on different historical subsets and lagged feature structures, thereby generating diverse sister forecasts that capture complementary temporal and meteorological patterns. These forecasts are subsequently aggregated using statistical averaging methods and a stacking ensemble meta-learner, which effectively exploits cross-model diversity. Comprehensive experiments conducted on the publicly available GEFCom2014 dataset demonstrate that the sister forecasts consistently outperform conventional decision tree and quantile regression benchmarks in both point and probabilistic forecasting. Moreover, the stacking ensemble achieves the lowest RMSE, CRPS, and Winkler scores, along with the most reliable calibration and sharper prediction intervals, leading to improved peak load estimation. The results confirm that the proposed sister-forecast ensemble framework not only enhances forecasting accuracy but also advances the state of the art in probabilistic short-Term load forecasting.  © 2025 IEEE.},
