	doi = {10.1109/ICMCTC62214.2025.11196204},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105022270444?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Electrical load denotes to the quantity of electrical power consumed by strategies and systems. Precise forecasting of electrical load is challenging because of its demanding, non-linear nature and unpredictability. By expanding superior artificial intelligence (AI) approaches, prediction accuracy and control grid strength can be improved efficiently. The research's aim is to incorporate a mathematical model-based method for forecasting electrical load in smart grids. A unique algorithm named Dynamic Red Deer optimization-driven Modified Support Vector Regression (DRD-MSVR) was suggested for accurate prediction of electrical load. The mathematical model is trained and evaluated using historical hourly load data. Z-score normalization is used to normalize the dataset and finish the data pre-processing. To extract the crucial characteristics from the processed data, the Kernel Principal Component Analysis (Kernel-PCA) technique is employed. The DRD method in our proposed predictive framework enhances the hyper-parameters of the MSVR, which improves the prediction reliability and convergence rate of the framework. The model can accomplish the unpredictable features in the electric load data due to the integration of DRD and MSVR. Python software is used for implementing the recommended model. In the importance evaluation phase, the overall performance of our mathematical model was analysed using many metrics. The outcomes demonstrate the effectiveness and distinction of the suggested methodology. This study combines the advantages of mathematical model-based methods with a robust approach for electric load forecasting in smart grids, addressing the growing need for effective energy management systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
