	abstract = {The electricity market is rapidly evolving by integrating renewable energy and smart grids, making accurate electricity price forecasting essential. This study introduces a novel three-stage model to enhance short-term electricity price forecasting. The first stage involves extensive data preprocessing to manage data noise and volatility, including aggregation, feature engineering, and normalization. The second stage employs an advanced feature selection method combining the Marine Predators Algorithm and Random Forest. This approach reduced dimensionality by 73% and improved model accuracy by 84% by identifying the most influential features. The final stage integrates a hybrid forecasting model that combines a Convolutional Neural Network with a Multi-Layer Perceptron, leveraging the strengths of both architectures. Bayesian Optimization is used to fine-tune hyperparameters for optimal performance. Implemented in MATLAB and tested on real-time data from Connecticut within ISO New England, the model showed superior results. For day-ahead forecasting, it achieved a Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) of 1.483%, a Daily Peak Mean Absolute Percentage Error (DP-MAPE) of 0.631%, a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of $0.410 per megawatt-hour, a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of $0.440 per megawatt-hour, and a coefficient of determination (R<sup>2</sup>) of 0.992. For week-ahead forecasting, it achieved a MAPE of 1.413%, a DP-MAPE of 1.460%, an MAE of $1.256 per megawatt-hour, an RMSE of $1.879 per megawatt-hour, and an R<sup>2</sup> of 0.999. These results demonstrate the robustness and accuracy of the model by outperforming reference models, providing precise short-term forecasts, and supporting informed decision-making. © 2025 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.},
