	abstract = {Modern energy systems are evolving into complex cyber-physical systems where agent-based control structures fundamentally depend on reliable communication networks for coordination and decision-making. This paper addresses the computational challenges of simulating communication networks in agent-based Cyber-Physical Energy Systems (CPES) by introducing a novel online meta-modeling approach. Current methods either sacrifice accuracy with simplified analytical models or demand significant computational resources with detailed simulations. Existing meta-models use stochastic traffic patterns that fail to capture the dynamic, application-specific communication behaviors characteristic of multi-agent energy systems and function as black-box predictors rather than integrated simulation models. Our approach employs a graph-based model of communication networks and a four-phase adaptive learning methodology specifically designed for complex communication patterns. The meta-model starts with pre-trained regressors based on historical data clusters and progressively incorporates online learning to adapt to scenario-specific agent interaction patterns observed during simulation execution. The methodology is demonstrated through a multi-agent reactive scheduling scenario, achieving substantial performance improvements over a detailed simulation approach. This enables computationally efficient analysis of agent-based energy systems without compromising communication modeling accuracy.  © 2025 IEEE.},
