	doi = {10.1109/ICPES68632.2025.11371805},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034881298?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Accurate cost estimation is critical for the effective planning and management of power transmission projects, particularly in complex geographic regions where design variability, high-dimensional feature spaces, intricate interdependencies among features, and highly non-linear relationships present significant challenges. This study proposes a hybrid global-local cost prediction framework that combines the strengths of model-driven and data-driven approaches. A novel local linear reconstruction model is developed, leveraging sparse supervised learning to assign feature-specific weights and mitigate the curse of dimensionality. Cost prediction is formulated as a convex optimization problem under the assumption of local linearity, allowing the model to estimate project costs as a linear combination of their nearest neighbors. To further enhance performance, the local model is integrated with global learners, namely LASSO and Random Forest, using a stacked ensemble method. This combination improves both predictive accuracy and generalizability by capturing both global patterns and local structural nuances. Empirical evaluation on a dataset of 146 real-world transmission projects in Guizhou Province, China, demonstrates that the proposed global-local model outperforms individual predictors, achieving a mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) of 7.21%. In addition to its predictive strength, the local model provides interpretable project similarity metrics, facilitating applications in cost simulation and project retrieval. The study also finds that extrapolation beyond the convex hull of known data significantly degrades performance, suggesting that the inclusion of more representative projects can further improve model robustness. © 2025 IEEE.},
