	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034765657?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Short-term power load forecasting is crucial for the stable operation and economic dispatching of power systems. This study addresses the unique dual-mode seasonal characteristics exhibited by power load data in the Panama region and proposes an optimized random forest prediction framework that combines data-driven seasonal decomposition with PCA. Preliminary research revealed that when the random forest model was applied directly, its performance on the test set was unsatisfactory, with a significant deviation between the predicted results and the actual values. Through in-depth data visualization analysis, we gained insight into systematic differences in the distribution patterns and influencing factors of load data between two time periods each year: January to April and May to December. Based on this finding, this study abandoned the traditional global modeling approach. Instead, it divided the dataset according to the aforementioned time periods and established independent prediction models for each subset. This segmentation strategy fundamentally improved the model performance. To further enhance prediction accuracy and suppress overfitting, we introduced PCA for feature dimensionality reduction based on the seasonal sub-models and used a grid search method to fine-tune the hyperparameters of the random forest. Ultimately, the optimized model achieved extremely high prediction accuracy on the test set. Experimental results demonstrate that this framework can effectively capture the complex intrinsic patterns of power load and provide a valuable reference solution for addressing timeseries prediction problems with similar characteristics. © 2025 IEEE.},
