	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031776221?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {With the deep integration of distributed renewable energy, electric vehicles, and demand-side flexible resources into smart grids, the power system is evolving toward higher complexity and stronger randomness. Against this backdrop, accurate ultra-short-term power load forecasting has become increasingly important for the stable operation and economic dispatch of the system. In response to the limitations of the kernel extreme learning machine (KELM) model based on the radial basis function (RBF) kernel, this paper proposes an innovative KELM model integrating the anisotropic RBF kernel (ARBF-KELM). It utilizes the Bayesian optimization algorithm for parameter optimization to build the BO-ARBF-KELM model. Based on 96-point data sets of two industries in a province in eastern China in September 2024, the effectiveness of the proposed model is verified. Empirical results show that the prediction performance of the BO-ARBF-KELM model is significantly better than that of other benchmark models in both one-step and three-step load forecasting. This superiority fully demonstrates the BOARBF-KELM model's excellent ability to capture the dynamic characteristics of smart grid loads. It provides reliable technical support for smart grid applications such as real-time optimal scheduling of distributed energy, precise triggering of demand response, and dynamic adjustment of energy storage operation strategies, thereby laying a solid foundation for the efficient, low-carbon, and safe operation of smart grids.  © 2025 IEEE.},
