	abstract = {Probabilistic Load Forecasting is critical for autonomous ships, as it quantifies future power demand fluctuations and provides essential support for energy management systems. This capability enables real-time autonomous optimization of generator set operations to enhance energy utilization efficiency in the absence of immediate human intervention, while ensuring continuous and stable power supply to mission-critical automated systems. To enhance the accuracy of probabilistic load forecasting for autonomous ships, this paper proposes a Gaussian Process model integrating Gaussian Mixture Modeling with Multi-point Interaction Analysis. The innovative contributions include: 1) designing a time-series feature extraction mechanism based on site interaction relationships; 2) constructing composite kernel functions adapted to power load characteristics; 3) employing Gaussian mixture distributions to model site coupling relationships, quantifying membership degrees through soft classification boundaries; and 4) introducing stochastic variational inference for parallel parameter optimization. The model offers four key advantages: it resolves feature dependencies through kernel matrices to precisely map trend, periodicity andinteraction patterns; employs soft boundaries to accurately characterize power station membership degrees and extract inter-site interaction features, effectively modeling ship grid loop characteristics; and significantly accelerates training via SVI's parallel framework.  © 2025 IEEE.},
