	abstract = {There is a growing number of Electric Vehicle (EV) charging points to keep up with the soaring demand of clean transportation. Nevertheless, random charging behavior leads to uncontrollable changes in loading that are a serious threat to energy distribution and power grid stability. In the present paper, an evolutionary model of predicting dynamic load in EV charging systems based on real time IoT sensor data and Support Vector Regression (SVR) is presented. The system comprises the integration of environmental, temporal and operational variables that include charging current, ambient temperature, plug-in time and battery state of charge collected via a distributed IoT sensor network. The determined data are pre-processed and utilized to train the SVR model to forecast the short-term load to the accuracy. The verification of the proposed system on real-time data measurements in a demonstration smart charging station proves that the system has a high level of accuracy with the mean absolute error (MAE) equal to 0.52 kW and R 2 equal to 0.93. A comparison of other machine learning models reveals that SVR is better at generalization and is efficient in dynamic settings. The system is scaleable and extends to heterogeneous EV fleets, opens up the prospects in the optimization in terms of load balancing and predictive energy scheduling in a smart grid.  © 2025 IEEE.},
