	abstract = {The maturation of carbon emissions trading markets, coupled with the strategic deployment of the energy internet, is elevating integrated energy systems (IES) to a pivotal role within modern power infrastructure. This research tackles the planning complexities inherent in IES by examining its multi-energy flow dynamics-spanning electrical, gaseous, and thermal domains-through a combined quantitative-qualitative lens that deciphers cross-system operational couplings. Crucially, the model incorporates the significant volatility observed in carbon market pricing. To this end, we introduce a novel expansion planning framework for regional IES that explicitly internalizes carbon price risk. Our methodology begins with a composite prediction technique, which integrates Fast Complementary Ensemble Empirical Mode Decomposition (FCEEMD) for signal processing, a Segmented Adaptive Grey Wolf Optimizer (SAGWO) for parameter calibration, and a Least Squares Support Vector Regression (LSSVR) core to generate accurate carbon price forecasts. These predictions subsequently inform a mean-reversion stochastic model, designed to characterize the probabilistic nature of future carbon trading prices. Finally, a series of case studies benchmark planning outcomes across diverse carbon market conditions, demonstrating that the proposed approach not only enhances decision-making robustness but also achieves significant improvements in both economic and environmental performance. © 2025 IEEE.},
