	abstract = {Accurate short-term and medium-term wind power forecasting (WPF) is essential for the stable and reliable integration of wind energy into modern power systems. While artificial intelligence (AI) has improved forecasting accuracy, the potential of Bayesian-optimized hybrid deep learning (DL) and machine learning (ML) models for WPF remains underexplored. This study introduces a novel approach that combines hybrid DL architectures with ML models, optimized using Bayesian Optimization (BO), to enhance prediction performance. The proposed framework integrates Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), and Residual Networks (ResNet) into a BO-CNN-ResNet-LSTM framework, capturing both spatial and temporal patterns. Alongside, traditional ML models are also optimized for comparison. The methodology is tested using historical wind power data from Pakistan for day-ahead and week-ahead forecasts. Results show that under limited data availability, the Bayesian-optimized Random Forest (BORF) model outperforms the hybrid DL model. For example, in Gul Ahmad WPP, BO-RF achieves a Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) of 0.0036% for day-ahead forecasting, compared to 0.0874% for the hybrid DL model. This suggests that ML models work better with smaller datasets due to their efficiency, while hybrid DL models excel with larger datasets by leveraging deep feature extraction. This study establishes a data-adaptive framework where ML is preferred for small datasets, and hybrid DL is more effective in data-rich environments, improving WPF reliability and supporting the smooth integration of wind energy into power grids.  © 2025 IEEE.},
