	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105030546337?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Existing ICS honeypots suffer from three critical shortcomings: they fail to preserve protocol-state fidelity, accurately emulate controller identities, or reproduce realistic control-business workflows. Consequently, sophisticated adversaries can easily detect and bypass them. To address these gaps, we introduce a multi-layered framework called ICSTrap. At the protocol level, ICSTrap incorporates clean-room state machines to enhance interactivity, while genuine industrial network traffic is replayed to lure attackers into realistic exchange patterns. By applying fuzzing techniques to many Programmable Logic Controller (PLC) models, ICSTrap harvests authentic identity data and then uses these identities to masquerade its protocol honeypots as real devices or to deploy PLC-specific traps. Beyond protocol emulation, ICSTrap recreates three enterprise-scale scenarios - power-grid dispatch, petrochemical control, and gaspipeline operations - by integrating conventional web honeypots with live control flows. In an 11-month, global deployment of 51 honeypots, ICSTrap recorded 2,259,265 connection attempts from 1,365 unique IP addresses. To extract high-threat events from this dataset, we developed an IP reputation scoring method that identified the top ten malicious sources, a tool-and-payload detection technique that revealed the five most commonly used probing frameworks, and a threat organization attribution approach that uncovered the five leading attacker groups.  © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Industrial Control System; Multi-layer Honeypot; Protocol Emulation; Threat Analysis},
