	abstract = {Weather conditions that frequently switch from bright to cloudy challenge solar energy systems, especially in areas where the sky is often unstable. Still, most solar trackers use algorithms or sensors that adjust based on fixed data, which means they lose much energy when the sun’s position changes. The challenge of inefficiency makes it tough for solar energy to excel as a reliable, renewable source for grid-constrained or remote sites. To solve this issue, the research suggests a new solar tracking system that uses machine learning to suggest the best panel position for every weather situation. This system uses a Random Forest Classifier to classify the weather live and a Gradient Boosted Regressor to know the amount of solar irradiance at several angles as the day progresses. An optimization layer uses the predictions to decide which angle to use for the photovoltaic panels each hour. Training and validation on previous weather and sunshine datasets are followed by simulation with real-time inputs over 30 days in various weather conditions. Days with mixed weather show the maximum difference, as the machine learning tracker improved energy capture by as much as 59% over the traditional static and dual-axis systems. Its ability to adjust and predict electric demand on the fly helped deal with noise from sensors and unexpected weather. This study shows that AI can improve the reliability of renewable energy and set up solar infrastructure that can cope with changing weather situations. © 2025 IEEE.},
