	abstract = {Accurate forecasting of wind power is crucial for maintaining grid stability, optimizing energy distribution, and minimizing financial risks associated with power imbalances. However, the inherent intermittency and stochastic nature of wind energy make prediction highly challenging. Traditional machine learning and statistical models, such as ARIMA and persistence models, often fail to capture complex temporal dependencies, leading to suboptimal forecasts. To overcome these limitations, this study proposes an advanced ensemble learning framework that integrates multiple predictive architectures, including Random Forest (RF), Gradient Boosting Machine (GBM), Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), and N-BEATS. The ensemble model leverages the unique strengths of each algorithm, improving generalization and reducing forecasting errors. Additionally, a penalty-aware loss function is incorporated to mitigate the financial impact of prediction errors, making the model more applicable for real-world energy markets. Experimental evaluations conducted on real-world wind power datasets demonstrate that the proposed ensemble approach significantly enhances forecasting accuracy, achieving the lowest RMSE (9.34 W), MAPE (3.45%), SMAPE (3.01%), and MAE (7.89 W) compared to standalone models. The model also achieves a 23.8% reduction in financial penalties, highlighting its economic viability. Comparative analysis with traditional forecasting methods further validates the robustness and scalability of the proposed framework. By providing a reliable, cost-effective, and highly adaptable forecasting solution, this study contributes to improving wind energy integration into modern power systems, ensuring more stable and efficient grid operations. © 2025 IEEE.},
