	abstract = {The power demand of the fuel cell hybrid electric vehicle (FCHEV) varies with vehicle’s driving conditions. Conventional energy management strategy (EMS) often demonstrates limited adaptability to dynamic driving conditions due to its fixed objective function. To this end, this paper proposes a machine learning-based strategy for driving condition recognition and energy management optimization of FCHEV. The EMS can recognize the driving condition of FCHEV in real time and choose the most suitable Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) model to optimize energy management. The EMS utilizes Random Forest (RF) algorithm to predict the power demand of the hybrid system and classify driving conditions. Subsequently, the Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (DDPG) algorithm is applied to optimize the energy management of each driving condition. These above steps ensure a real-time, efficient, and adaptive energy management strategy. Simulation results demonstrate that RF exhibits excellent fitting capability and robustness across a substantial dataset with its R<sup>2</sup> more than 0.9975 during the predicting process and the accuracy more than 0.98 in the process of driving condition recognition. Additionally, DDPG effectively converges under various driving conditions, delivering optimal energy management solutions that significantly reduce hydrogen consumption during operation and markedly mitigate fuel cell (FC) lifespan degradation. Compared to rule-based strategy, the proposed EMS reduces hydrogen consumption by up to 10.5% and extends FC lifespan by up to 19.1%, thereby greatly enhancing the power system’s stability and efficiency. © 2025 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.},
