	abstract = {With the continuous advancement of intelligent power systems, load forecasting plays an increasingly important role in ensuring the safe operation of the power grid and enabling optimal resource scheduling. To address the limitations of traditional forecasting models in capturing the nonlinear characteristics and dynamic variations of load data, this paper proposes three hybrid load forecasting approaches: a deep fusion model combining Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks and LightGBM, a stacking regression model based on Support Vector Regression (SVR) and XGBoost, and an incremental learning model built on the River framework. Under an experimental setting with a global data split of 78% for training and 22% for testing, the River-based incremental model demonstrated the best performance, achieving an R2 of 0.97, with a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 3253.07 kW and a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 4258.53 kW, indicating excellent trend-fitting capability and predictive accuracy. The LSTM + LightGBM model followed closely with an R2 of 0.94, making it suitable for tasks requiring high-precision load forecasting. In contrast, the SVR + XGBoost model, while achieving a lower R2 of 0.78, features a simpler structure and offers advantages in model interpretability and deployment efficiency. The experimental results suggest that hybrid models are well-suited for long-term, high-accuracy load modeling tasks, while the River-based incremental learning model is more appropriate for real-time forecasting and stream data applications. These methods provide diverse and reliable modeling strategies to meet varying practical demands in power load forecasting.  © 2025 IEEE.},
