	abstract = {The accurate forecasting of electrical load in short-term intervals is crucial for the efficient and secure operation of modern power systems. The dynamic nature of energy demand necessitates sophisticated forecasting techniques that can adapt to real-time changes, thereby optimizing power generation and distribution. This study proposes a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model optimized using Hyperparameter Optimization (HPO) to address the inherent non-linearities in load data and enhance predictive performance. The LSTM with HPO achieved a Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) of 2.63%, substantially outperforming other models such as k-Nearest Neighbors (3.77%), Random Forest (3.46%), and Artificial Neural Networks (3.58%). It also recorded superior performance in Mean Squared Error (MSE) and Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), with values of 1.1423 and 1.0688, and an R2 of 0.9442. Additionally, experiments were conducted combining LSTM with other models to form hybrid architectures without applying HPO. These LSTM-based hybrids, such as RF-LSTM, consistently outperformed non-LSTM models, achieving a MAPE as low as 2.81%. © 2025 IEEE.},
