	doi = {10.1515/9783111264271-041},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85203102501?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Amidst the global pursuit of climate protection goals, the prominence of hydrogen technologies has surged, and Germany stands at the forefront of this transformative journey. In 2020, the German federal government published the National Hydrogen Strategy, which includes potentials for coupling with the industrial and transportation sectors, as well as some relevant interfaces to the power sector. This Strategy is a blueprint that not only illustrated national commitment of Germany but also provides a template for international emulation. Thus, hydrogen-based technology concepts could be used, among other things, for storing excess renewable energy from solar and wind installations or for stabilizing power grids in network operation. However, there is currently a lot of uncertainty among stakeholders regarding the future use of green hydrogen and its production using the power-to-gas process, as corresponding plants have only been installed to a limited extent (as of the time of printing in 2023, 36 power-to-gas plants are in operation in Germany) and a corresponding hydrogen infrastructure does not yet exist. This chapter therefore provides an overview of the current status of the technology and shows to what extent the use of hydrogen could influence the planning and future operation of power grids. Furthermore, the current political and regulatory framework conditions in this context are explained. Finally, it is shown how a potential hydrogen market could develop in Germany in the near future, and what opportunities for optimizing the energy system in terms of climate protection could arise from this. © 2024 Walter de Gruyter GmbH, Berlin/Boston. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Electrolysis; Hydrogen; Hydrogen network; Hydrogen regulation; National Hydrogen Strategy; Power-to-gas},
