	doi = {10.1109/ACOIT62457.2024.10939156},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105002724855?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {In recent years, the integration of photovoltaic (PV) systems into the power grid has seen significant growth, driven by the global demand for renewable energy sources. However, the efficiency of PV systems is highly dependent on the capability of the Maximum Power Point Tracking (MPPT) algorithms to adapt to varying environmental conditions such as partial shading. Traditional MPPT techniques like Perturb and Observe (P&O) or Incremental Conductance (INC) often struggle with the complex power-voltage characteristics under these conditions, leading to suboptimal performance. This study explores the application of Support Vector Regression (SVR) for enhancing the MPPT process in PV systems. By leveraging the powerful regression capabilities of SVR, the proposed algorithm accurately predicts the maximum power point (MPP) in real-time, even in the presence of multiple local maxima caused by partial shading. The methodology involves training an SVR model using historical PV data, where the model learns the relationship between voltage and power. The trained SVR model is then employed in the MPPT process to predict the power output for different voltage levels and guide the system to the global MPP. The simulation results demonstrate that the SVR-based MPPT algorithm significantly outperforms traditional methods, achieving faster convergence to the MPP and maintaining stable power output under dynamic shading conditions. Detailed analysis of PV characteristic curves, SVR model training, and real-time MPPT performance confirms the robustness and reliability of the proposed approach. Furthermore, the adaptability of the SVR model to learn from new data ensures that the MPPT algorithm remains effective over time, accommodating variations in irradiance, temperature, and shading patterns. This study concludes that integrating SVR into MPPT algorithms can substantially enhance the efficiency and reliability of PV systems, making it a promising solution for the challenges posed by partial shading and other environmental factors. © 2024 IEEE.},
