	abstract = {Artificial intelligence plays a significant influence in raising living standards. Fog computing, mist computing, edge computing, software-defined network, Internet of Things, digital twin, and Industrial 4.0 are just a few forms of cloud computing technology. A cyber-physical system’s (CPS’s) data stream contains a significant volume of data, which is analyzed and interpreted using machine-learning (ML) methods. Cyber elements such as computation, software, and networking are combined with designed elements such as physical processes and human factors in the CPS. It incorporates loops between computing with reciprocal feedback, physical processes, sensors, actuators, monitoring, and control elements, due to networked physical components and integrated processes. ML approaches are used in three domains in the CPS: smart grid, transportation, and manufacturing industry. Electrical power prediction, short-term load forecasting, blackout warning, energy demand, and economic load dispatch are all aspects of the smart grid. In the case of transportation, domain issues are driver monitoring and analysis, traffic accident obstacle classification, traffic congestion, pollution level prediction, and so on. Metal cutting, tool wear/error detection, semiconductor manufacturing, inline quality prediction, source diagnostics, bearing fault diagnosis and identify machine failure, etc. These concerns exist in the manufacturing domain. Various ML methods are used in CPS to address these challenges. The generalized linear model, multilayer perceptron, Bayesian network, random forest, support vector machines, decision tree, self-organizing map, naive Bayes, particle swarm optimization, hidden Markov model, and Q-learning are the main ML techniques used in CPS to improve accuracy. © 2023 selection and editorial matter, Anil Kumar Sagar, Parma Nand, Neetesh Kumar, Sanjoy Das and Subrata Sahana; individual chapters, the contributors.},
