	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105000145671?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The roll out of new satellite systems, the capacity expansion of existing satellite constellations, and new general-purpose services available on demand are paving the way for new business opportunities and beneficial technical solutions in many industrial use cases. Service providers seeking broad coverage or high redundancy for their systems are already preparing strategies for the integration of satellite communication, in their industrial systems such as future mobility and transportation systems (for both passengers and cargo transportation). However, technology updates, optimizations, or even replacements are complex and require careful impact assessment of new features and performance when operating over a satellite network. This work provides a reality check on a critical use case for railway systems, exploring distinct deployment considerations for the satellite network. This use case deals with reliable control of trains and of the railway infrastructure, as well as on a seamless inter-operation of different subsystems to ensure proper operation of the system. In order to reduce the assessment complexity and its inherent costs, a simulation-based framework is used to analyze the corresponding application scenarios over satellites on system level. This framework acts as a digital twin for the satellite system and provides subsystem simulation/emulation capabilities. It allows satellite constellation, mobility, and communication parameters to be estimated, and results to be reproduced and validated over different scenarios, each with different topology and/or configuration. The insights of this work provide a basis for the design of future satellite system-based industrial services and the results contribute to a better understanding of the impact of different satellite constellations, their capabilities, as well as their performance with respect to requirements of critical railway services and other industrial sectors. Copyright © 2024 by the International Astronautical Federation (IAF). All rights reserved.},
