	abstract = {The energy demand grows with the increase in population. This makes the society to additionally rely upon alternative or renewable sources of energy. Out of the existing renewable energy sources, solar energy grabs world-wide attention. This is because of its freely abundant availability, clean and easier harnessing capability. The development of solar PV installation can reduce the energy demand. However, their inherent power generation characteristic causes great difficulties to grid operators in balancing the supply and demand. Thus, in order to achieve reliable operation of power system, the knowledge on operational performance indices of solar photovoltaic distribution system lies essential. This further claim the dependence of predictive technologies. Prediction models help in quantifying the unique performance indicators of grid connected PV system. The advantages of employing predictive technologies include improvement in the reliability of power system operation and gains effective installation. It is not just important to set up PV plants, with the availability of space. However, successful installation happens only when it's annual energy generation, yield, performance ratio and capacity factor are pre-determined pertaining to the locality. Furthermore, International Energy Agency has developed several objectives supporting the photovoltaic's energy system programme. One such objective is task 13, which aims to improve the operation and reliability of PV power system by essentially monitoring its performance on-field. Hence the estimation of operational performance indices of a realistic PV plant terms important. The current approaches which are employed for prediction of AC energy generation include physics based model (numerical weather prediction), statistical models (artificial neural network and time-series), fuzzy based approach and hybrid structures which include the combination of above stated models. On the other hand, Response Surface Methodology (RSM) provides an interactive platform for optimization of response variable affected by several independent input variables. Furthermore, RSM is a software based design which helps in quantifying the desired response. Hence a response surface based design for predicting the PV performance indicators is adopted. Response surface based approach is not reported in literatures in its application to the prediction of realistic performance of grid connected PV system. The training inputs to build the RSM model is obtained from the reported grid connected case studies in the literature. In addition, the real time data inputs of a 5 MWp and 100 kWp PV systems are also tested for repeatability of the developed model. Furthermore, a decision on possible combination of input factors yielding highly acceptable prediction accuracy can be easily made. Thus, an effective design tool employable by solar design engineers and grid operators for decision making is proposed. © 2018 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.},
