	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85174659038?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Due to lack of online operation data, offline operation data under the worst circumstance are generally used for modelling of large UHVDC systems for its accuracy in emulation of the boundary of the power system as well as situations under other circumstances. However, online operation data may better represent the operation state in most cases, especially for robust grids which, at most of time, are not likely to operate under the boundary circumstance. To study the issue in more details, using both online and offline data, this work investigates and compares the accuracy of the electromechanical-electromagnetic modelling of a large AC-DC interconnected grid with over 20000 buses and 9 HVDC/UHVDC transmission systems, where the HVDC/UHVDC systems are modelled in details on both electrical components and control systems. The simulation results in both cases are compared with real data collected from the dispatching centre and the substation, during which the modelling in both cases are validated. In addition, based on the type of operation data provided and the purpose for modelling, a probabilistic method for applicability assessment is proposed. For purpose of long-term planning, off-line data are more applicable; for short-term control, however, the applicability depends on the probability of the operation state near the operation boundary. For power system with low-probability near-boundary operations, online data are more applicable. © 2020 The Institution of Engineering and Technology},
	author_keywords = {APPLICABILITY; INTERCONNECTED AC-DC GRIDS; MULTI-TIME STEP HYBRID SIMULATION; OFFLINE AND ONLINE OPERATION DATA; PROSPECT IN CYBER-PHYSICAL SYSTEMS},
