	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85084245055?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {An operational management system of residential energy supply networks employing power and heat interchanges among energy supply devices is developed by hierarchically integrating energy demand prediction, operation planning, and operation control with optimization approaches. The residential energy demands for 24 h ahead are predicted by using support vector regression with occupant behavior information as well as weather forecast and energy demand history. The optimal operation planning for 24 h ahead, which includes the on–off schedule of energy supply devices and the heat interchange schedule, is determined using a mixed-integer linear programming approach. The predicted energy demands and the current operating status and stored energies are inputted to the optimal operation planning problem as an initial condition. The actual operation of energy supply devices and heat interchange follows the derived operation schedule and predefined rule. The energy demand prediction and optimal operation planning are updated during the day by using a receding horizon approach. The developed operational management system is applied to a residential energy supply network consisting fuel cell-based cogeneration units in a housing complex. Annual operating simulation under measured energy demands reveals a fine behavior of heat interchange among storage tanks and an increase in the energy-saving effect by introducing the heat interchange in comparison with the power interchange operation and the separate operation of fuel cell-based cogeneration units. © 2016 University of Ljubljana.},
